# Windows holding the system's own widgets, in the manner of wxWidgets: a
# button is an `NSButton` on macOS, a Win32 button on Windows, a GTK button
# on Linux, never one this package draws, so a program looks and behaves as
# the system's own programs do, its keys, its accessibility and its
# languages included. The program makes windows, lays widgets out in them by
# rows, columns and grids, asks `wait` for what the person did, and changes
# its own state by it.
#
# What it does NOT do: draw a widget of its own, or anything into a window
# (a drawing area is pending); a full-screen program in a terminal (`tui`);
# decode an image file (`image`'s subpackages, whose `image.Image` a
# `picture` shows); show a web page, its assets and its bridge
# (`ui/webview`, whose web view is a widget of this package's app).
#
# **No callbacks: the program asks.** The language has no closures, so no
# widget calls the program back. `wait` runs the system's loop for a bounded
# time and answers the next `Event`, a variant naming the widget by the
# handle the program holds; the program matches it, changes its own state,
# and sets the widgets that show it. Nothing the program sets is answered as
# an event: only what the person does is.
#
# **A widget is a handle with a life.** An `App` is a handle of the platform
# layer, begun by `open` and ended by `close`, and one nobody closes stops
# the program when `main` returns, as every handle does (spec § 13). A
# `Widget` is a number its app gave it, never an address: its life begins at
# its maker and ends at `destroy`, of itself or of the box or window holding
# it, or at `close`; every call on a widget whose life has ended fails
# `closed`, and none can reach another widget, since an app never gives a
# number twice and a widget of another app is `invalid`. A widget is not a
# handle of spec § 13 because its window ends it: § 13's `transfers` would
# end the program's own name for a widget handed to its window, and the
# program names its widgets after that, in every event.
#
# **One thread.** AppKit, Win32 and GTK each hold the user interface on one
# thread, and so does this package: an app opens only on the thread `main`
# runs on, and `open` from another thread fails `unsupported`. An `App` is a
# value no thread can be handed (a thread takes an `i64` or a `str`), so
# every call on it is on that thread. Another thread's work reaches the user
# interface through what the program shares with it, read when a `timer`
# fires; the mailbox of feature 2647 will be the way once it lands.
#
# **The layout is computed here, the same on every platform**, in the
# manner of wxWidgets' sizers: a window lays its children out in a column,
# a `row` side by side, a `column` one under another, a `grid` in cells.
# Each widget needs the size the system draws it at, or the least the
# program asks (`Fit`), with its margin; along its box's line the room left
# is shared among the children that stretch, by their weights, and across
# it a child fills the room or stands at the start, the middle or the end. A
# window is never smaller than its content needs. The layout runs at each
# `wait`, `render` and `rect_of` after a change, and the native side only
# places each widget at the rectangle it gives, in points from the top-left
# corner of the window's content.
#
# **Two sides, one program.** On the native side the widgets are the
# system's; on the headless side they are only this package's data, drawn
# by fixed rules (`render` says which), and what the person does comes from
# the program itself, through `inject`, one input at each `wait`. A
# program's tests and a demo run headless and print the same transcript on
# every machine, with no screen; `open(.automatic)` takes the native side
# where the system offers one and the headless side where it does not. The
# native side is AppKit on macOS, Win32's own controls on Windows, and GTK 4
# on Linux and every other system, opened where it is installed and a
# display is named.
#
# **The backend's interface** is the platform layer's `hero_ui.h`, one shape
# for every system: a widget made by its number, its kind and its window,
# set, placed at a rectangle, read back, destroyed, and the queue of what
# the person did, each event naming a number; every other side (Win32, GTK,
# a web view) implements those calls and nothing of this module.
#
# It fails with `closed`, `invalid`, `timeout`, `unsupported`, `exists`,
# `limit` and `failed` (README § A4).
#
# Not written yet, each a `pending` block of its ledger: a thread's message to
# the user interface, through feature 2647's mailbox; a box drawn with a title
# and a frame around it; a combo box the person may type into; a number
# typed, or stepped up and down; a line between the widgets of a box; an area
# the program draws into, with its keys and its mouse; a tip shown over a
# widget; a date, a colour or a font chosen in the system's own picker; an
# icon in the system's tray or menu bar, and its notifications; a file
# dragged onto a window.

use bytes
use image
use time
use ui/hero_ui
use ui/internal/clips
use ui/internal/desk
use ui/internal/dialogs
use ui/internal/failure
use ui/internal/fixture
use ui/internal/frames
use ui/internal/keyboard
use ui/internal/loop
use ui/internal/makers
use ui/internal/menus
use ui/internal/model
use ui/internal/render as drawing
use ui/internal/state
use ui/internal/tables
use ui/internal/trees

## Codes

# The app is closed, or the widget's life has ended: destroyed, its window
# or its box destroyed, or its app closed; and a headless app whose injected
# inputs are spent while no timer runs. Every function on an app or a
# widget fails with it.
constant CLOSED: str
    failure.CLOSED

# An argument the call cannot take: a size or a weight outside its range, a
# text holding a zero byte, a widget of another app or of a kind the call
# does not set, an index past the items. Every function that takes one fails
# with it.
constant INVALID: str
    failure.INVALID

# Nothing happened in the time `wait` was given.
constant TIMEOUT: str
    failure.TIMEOUT

# The system has no native user interface this package reaches, or the call
# came from a thread other than `main`'s. `open` fails with it, and so does
# a native call the system offers no path for.
constant UNSUPPORTED: str
    failure.UNSUPPORTED

# A native app is open already in this process. `open` fails with it.
constant EXISTS: str
    failure.EXISTS

# The system has no memory or no room for what was asked. The makers and
# `open` fail with it.
constant LIMIT: str
    failure.LIMIT

# The system refused for a reason none of the above names; the message
# carries its words. Every call that reaches the native side fails with it.
constant FAILED: str
    failure.FAILED

## The app

# The most points a size, a position, a spacing, a padding or a margin
# takes, on both axes: a million, past every screen.
constant MOST_SIZE: i64
    1000000

# The highest weight a widget, a column or a row stretches by.
constant MOST_STRETCH: i64
    1000

# Which side an app opens on.
variant Backend
    # The system's own widgets where this machine offers them to this
    # session, the headless side where it does not.
    automatic
    # The system's own widgets, or `unsupported`.
    native
    # This package's data alone, drawn as text by `render`.
    headless

# An open user interface: its windows, its widgets and their state, made by
# `open` and ended by `close`. It is an address of the platform layer, so a
# program holds one in one place and hands it on with `@`; a copy made
# before `close` holds a handle whose life has ended, and handing it to any
# function here aborts (spec § 13).
record App
    # The floor's handle, `nullptr` once `close` has emptied this place.
    handle: hero_ui.App
    # The layer's number for the app, which each of its widgets carries.
    number: i64
    # Whether the app stands on the system's own widgets.
    native: bool
    # The widgets as data. Only this package reads it.
    table: model.Model
    # The icon the program gave the app, kept on both sides.
    icon: desk.Icon
    # What the program last put on the clipboard, kept on both sides: the
    # headless side's clipboard, which a read answers from.
    clipboard: Clip
    # The inputs `inject` queued and `wait` has not given yet, in order.
    inputs: [Input]
    # The events made and not yet answered by `wait`, in order.
    events: [Event]
    # The answers `inject` queued for message boxes, in order.
    answers: [Answer]
    # The choices `inject` queued for file dialogs, in order.
    choices: [Choice]
    # The app's clock in nanoseconds since it opened. A headless app's moves
    # only as far as `wait` lets time pass, so its timers fire on the same
    # waits on every machine; a native app's follows the monotonic clock.
    clock: i64
    # The monotonic clock's reading when a native app opened.
    opened: time.Mark

# A new app on `backend`.
#
# - `backend`: `.native` for the system's own widgets, `.headless` for this
#   package's data alone, `.automatic` for the first where this machine
#   offers it and the second where it does not.
#
# Fails `unsupported` where `.native` was asked of a machine with no native
# side for this session, or where the calling thread is not `main`'s;
# `exists` where a native app is open already; `limit`. See also `close`.
function open(backend: Backend) -> App?
    return makers.open(backend)

# Closes `app`: every window and widget it holds is destroyed, every queue
# emptied, and the place emptied, so every later call through it fails
# `closed`.
#
# - `app`: the caller's place holding the app; emptied whatever the answer.
#
# Fails `closed` where it is closed already. See also `open`.
function close(@app: App) -> ()?
    return makers.close(@app)

# Whether `app` stands on the system's own widgets.
#
# - `app`: the app asked.
function is_native(app: App) -> bool
    return app.native

## Widgets

# A widget, a box, a window, a menu, a menu item or a timer of an app: a
# number its app gave it, which every event about it names. Made only by
# this package's makers; compare two with `==`.
record Widget
    # The number of the app that made it.
    app: i64
    # The app's number for it, from 1, never given twice by one app.
    id: i64

# What a widget is.
variant Kind
    # A top-level window, laying its children out in a column.
    window
    # A box laying its children out side by side.
    row
    # A box laying its children out one under another.
    column
    # A box laying its children out in cells, row after row.
    grid
    # A text the person reads.
    label
    # A button the person presses.
    button
    # One line of text the person types.
    text_field
    # Many lines of text the person types.
    text_area
    # A box the person checks and unchecks, with its text.
    checkbox
    # Items of which the person chooses one, each with its round button.
    radio_group
    # Items in a box, of which the person chooses one, or several.
    list
    # Items in a button that opens them, of which the person chooses one.
    combo
    # A bar filled as far as a value goes.
    progress
    # A knob the person drags between two values.
    slider
    # An image shown.
    picture
    # A menu of a window's menu bar.
    menu
    # An item of a menu.
    menu_item
    # A line between a menu's items.
    menu_separator
    # A clock that fires at a period.
    timer
    # The system's web view, made by `ui/webview`'s `webview`.
    webview
    # Pages chosen by tabs.
    tabs
    # A page of tabs.
    page
    # A box whose content scrolls.
    scrolled
    # Two panes and the divider between them.
    split
    # A pane of a split.
    pane
    # Rows of cells in columns.
    table
    # Items under items, folded and unfolded.
    tree
    # A menu shown where the person right-clicks a widget.
    context_menu

# What `widget` is.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: the widget asked about.
#
# Fails `closed` where its life has ended, `invalid` where it is another
# app's.
function kind_of(app: App, widget: Widget) -> Kind?
    return state.kind_of(app, widget: widget)

# Destroys `widget` and everything it holds: a window's widgets and menus,
# a box's widgets, a menu's items; a timer stops.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: the widget destroyed; every later call on it, or on what it
#   held, fails `closed`.
#
# Fails `closed` where its life has ended already, `invalid` where it is
# another app's, `failed` where the system refused.
function destroy(@app: App, widget: Widget) -> ()?
    return makers.destroy(@app, widget: widget)

# A rectangle in a window's content, or a page's, a scrolled box's or a
# pane's, in points from its top-left corner, the y axis down.
record Rect
    # The left edge.
    x: i64
    # The top edge.
    y: i64
    # The width, 0 or more.
    width: i64
    # The height, 0 or more.
    height: i64

# Where `widget` stands in its window's content, or in the content of the
# page, scrolled box or pane it stands in, laid out first where anything
# changed; for a window, its content's size at 0, 0; for a page or a pane,
# where its tabs or its split shows it.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it; its windows are laid out where needed.
# - `widget`: a window, or a widget or a box a window holds.
#
# A hidden widget answers where it stood last. Fails `closed`, `invalid`
# for a menu, a menu item or a timer, which stand in no window's content.
function rect_of(@app: App, widget: Widget) -> Rect?
    return state.rect_of(@app, widget: widget)

## Windows

# A new window, titled `title`, its content `width` by `height` points or
# what it needs if that is more. It lays its children out in a column, with
# `model.WINDOW_PADDING` points inside its edges; it is shown at the next
# `wait`, laid out, unless hidden first.
#
# - `app`: the app it is made in.
# - `title`: its title, without a zero byte.
# - `width`: its content's width in points, 0 to `MOST_SIZE`.
# - `height`: its content's height in points, 0 to `MOST_SIZE`.
#
# Fails `invalid`, `closed` where `app` is, `limit` and `failed`.
function window(@app: App, title: str, width: i64, height: i64) -> Widget?
    return makers.window(@app, title: title, width: width, height: height)

# Gives window `widget` a content of `width` by `height` points, or what it
# needs if that is more. No event comes of it.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: the window.
# - `width`: points, 0 to `MOST_SIZE`.
# - `height`: points, 0 to `MOST_SIZE`.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `failed`.
function set_size(@app: App, widget: Widget, width: i64, height: i64) -> ()?
    return state.set_size(@app, window: widget, width: width, height: height)

# Lets the person resize window `widget`, or keeps its size.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: the window.
# - `on`: true to let the person resize it, as a new window does.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `failed`.
function set_resizable(@app: App, widget: Widget, on: bool) -> ()?
    return state.set_resizable(@app, window: widget, on: on)

## Windows on the screen

# A screen of the system: its whole area and its work area, the whole less
# the menu bar, the Dock or the task bar, in the desktop's points, whose
# origin is the top-left corner of the system's main screen and whose y axis
# goes down; and its scale.
record Screen
    # Its whole area.
    area: Rect
    # The part of it a window is given room in.
    work: Rect
    # The pixels a point takes on each axis, times 100: 100 on an ordinary
    # screen, 200 on a Retina one.
    scale: i64

# Where a window stands: the screen it stands on most, and its frame on that
# screen, its title bar and its border included.
record Frame
    # The screen, from 0, the system's main one.
    screen: i64
    # The frame's left edge from the screen's left edge, in points.
    x: i64
    # The frame's top edge from the screen's top edge, in points, the y axis
    # down.
    y: i64
    # The frame's width, in points.
    width: i64
    # The frame's height, in points, its title bar included.
    height: i64

# The state a window stands in.
variant WindowState
    # Shown at the size the program or the person gave it.
    normal
    # Minimized into the Dock or the task bar.
    minimized
    # Maximized over its screen's work area; zoomed, on macOS.
    maximized
    # Filling its screen with no frame; on macOS in a space of its own.
    full_screen

# What stands behind a window's widgets.
variant Backdrop
    # The system's plain window background.
    plain
    # The system's translucent material, which blurs what stands behind the
    # window: the under-window material on macOS, Mica on Windows 11.
    translucent

# Every screen of the system, the main one first (on macOS the one holding
# the menu bar, on Windows the primary monitor), in the order the system
# lists them.
#
# - `app`: the app asking.
#
# The headless side has one screen of `ui/internal/desk`'s size, 1920 by
# 1080 points at scale 100, its work area all of it but a task bar 40
# points high along its bottom. Fails `closed`, `failed`. On Windows and
# Linux the native side is pending: `unsupported`.
function screens(app: App) -> [Screen]?
    return desk.screens(app)

# Where window `widget` stands: the screen it stands on most and its frame
# there, laid out and shown first as `wait` would.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: the window.
#
# A window the program never placed stands where the system first put it;
# the headless side puts it in the middle of its screen's work area. Fails
# `closed`, `invalid` for a widget no window, `failed`; `unsupported` where
# the system offers no position, as Wayland's compositors do not.
function frame_of(@app: App, widget: Widget) -> Frame?
    return desk.frame_of(@app, window: widget)

# Moves window `widget` so its frame's top-left corner stands at `x`, `y` on
# screen `screen`. No event comes of it.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: the window, shown or not; a window placed before it is first
#   shown appears there.
# - `screen`: a screen of `screens`, from 0.
# - `x`: points from the screen's left edge, `-MOST_SIZE` to `MOST_SIZE`.
# - `y`: points from the screen's top edge, the same range.
#
# The system may keep part of the frame on a screen: macOS keeps a title bar
# below the menu bar. Fails `closed`, `invalid` for no such screen or a
# position out of its range, `failed`; `unsupported` where the system lets
# no program place its windows, as Wayland's compositors do not. See also
# `frame_of`, `center`.
function set_position(@app: App, widget: Widget, screen: i64, x: i64, y: i64) -> ()?
    return desk.set_position(@app, window: widget, screen: screen, x: x, y: y)

# Centres window `widget` in the work area of the screen it stands on. No
# event comes of it.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it; its windows are laid out first.
# - `widget`: the window.
#
# A window larger than the work area stands at its top-left corner. Fails
# `closed`, `invalid`, `failed`, `unsupported` as `set_position` does. See
# also `center_over`.
function center(@app: App, widget: Widget) -> ()?
    return desk.centered(@app, window: widget, over: 0)

# Centres window `widget` over window `over`'s frame, kept within the work
# area of the screen `over` stands on: a dialog over its document. No event
# comes of it.
#
# - `app`: the app holding them; its windows are laid out first.
# - `widget`: the window moved.
# - `over`: another window of the app.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid` where `over` is `widget` or no window, `failed`,
# `unsupported` as `set_position` does. See also `center`.
function center_over(@app: App, widget: Widget, over: Widget) -> ()?
    return desk.centered_over(@app, window: widget, over: over)

# Brings window `widget` to `state`, leaving the one it stands in first, and
# returns once the system has made it. The content's new size it takes comes
# as `.resized`, as any resize does; the state itself gives no event.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it; its windows are laid out and shown first.
# - `widget`: the window.
# - `to`: the state; `.normal` gives back the size and the place it had
#   before it was maximized or put in full screen.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `failed` where the system did not make it within
# five seconds; `unsupported` where the system cannot, as a Wayland
# compositor cannot restore a minimized window. See also `state_of`.
function set_state(@app: App, widget: Widget, to: WindowState) -> ()?
    return desk.set_state(@app, window: widget, state: to)

# The state window `widget` stands in now, as the system holds it, the
# person's last act included.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: the window.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `failed`; `unsupported` where the system will
# not tell, as Wayland does not tell a minimized window. See also
# `set_state`.
function state_of(app: App, widget: Widget) -> WindowState?
    return desk.state_of(app, window: widget)

# Keeps window `widget` above every other application's windows, or lets it
# stand among them, as a new window does.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: the window.
# - `on`: true to keep it on top.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `failed`; `unsupported` where the system has no
# such level, as Wayland has none.
function set_on_top(@app: App, widget: Widget, on: bool) -> ()?
    return desk.set_on_top(@app, window: widget, on: on)

# Gives window `widget` its frame, a title bar with its buttons and a
# border, as a new window has, or takes them away, its content keeping its
# size; a window with no frame is moved by a drag on its background.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: the window.
# - `on`: true for a frame.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `failed` for a window in full screen, which
# keeps its frame until it leaves.
function set_framed(@app: App, widget: Widget, on: bool) -> ()?
    return desk.set_framed(@app, window: widget, on: on)

# Sets how opaque window `widget` is, its widgets with it.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: the window.
# - `opacity`: from 0, wholly see-through, to 255, wholly opaque, as a new
#   window is.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid` for an opacity out of its range, `failed`.
function set_opacity(@app: App, widget: Widget, opacity: i64) -> ()?
    return desk.set_opacity(@app, window: widget, opacity: opacity)

# Puts `backdrop` behind window `widget`'s widgets.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: the window.
# - `backdrop`: the backdrop; `.plain` as a new window has.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `failed`; `unsupported` where the system has no
# translucent material.
function set_backdrop(@app: App, widget: Widget, backdrop: Backdrop) -> ()?
    return desk.set_backdrop(@app, window: widget, backdrop: backdrop)

# Gives the app its icon: the one the Dock shows on macOS, and every window's
# and the task bar's on Windows.
#
# - `app`: the app.
# - `img`: the image, of any format, at most 1024 by 1024 pixels.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid` for an image that is not valid or too large,
# `failed`; `unsupported` where the system takes an icon only from the
# program's files, as GTK 4 does.
function set_icon(@app: App, img: image.Image) -> ()?
    return desk.set_icon(@app, img: img)

# Makes window `widget` modal: until the program ends it or destroys the
# window, the person acts on it alone and the app's other windows take no
# input, as a dialog of the program's own needs. The program goes on asking
# `wait`; the window is brought to the front. One window at a time.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it; its windows are laid out and shown first.
# - `widget`: the window.
# - `on`: true to make it modal, false to end it.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `failed` where another window is modal already.
function set_modal(@app: App, widget: Widget, on: bool) -> ()?
    return desk.set_modal(@app, window: widget, on: on)

## Layout

# A new box in `into` that lays its children out side by side, from the
# left, `model.SPACING` points between two.
#
# - `app`: the app it is made in.
# - `into`: a window, a row, a column, a grid, a page, a scrolled box or a
#   pane.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid` where `into` holds no widgets.
function row(@app: App, into: Widget) -> Widget?
    return makers.make(@app, into: into, kind: hero_ui.HERO_UI_ROW, text: "", items: [])

# A new box in `into` that lays its children out one under another, from
# the top, `model.SPACING` points between two.
#
# - `app`: the app it is made in.
# - `into`: a window, a row, a column, a grid, a page, a scrolled box or a
#   pane.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid` where `into` holds no widgets.
function column(@app: App, into: Widget) -> Widget?
    return makers.make(@app, into: into, kind: hero_ui.HERO_UI_COLUMN, text: "", items: [])

# A new box in `into` that lays its children out in cells, `columns` to a
# row, row after row; each column as wide as its widest child, each row as
# high as its highest.
#
# - `app`: the app it is made in.
# - `into`: a window, a row, a column, a grid, a page, a scrolled box or a
#   pane.
# - `columns`: the cells of a row, 1 to `MOST_STRETCH`.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`. See also `set_grid_stretch`.
function grid(@app: App, into: Widget, columns: i64) -> Widget?
    return makers.grid(@app, into: into, columns: columns)

# New tabs in `into`: pages one at a time, each chosen by its tab, as a
# settings window shows them, wxWidgets' `wxNotebook`. They need the most
# any page needs and the room their tabs and border take; every page is laid
# out in the same room, the one chosen shown. `set_selection` and
# `selection_of` choose and read the page shown by its index, in the order
# made; the person choosing a tab is `.selection_changed`.
#
# - `app`: the app it is made in.
# - `into`: a window, a row, a column, a grid, a page, a scrolled box or a
#   pane.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `failed`. See also `page`.
function tabs(@app: App, into: Widget) -> Widget?
    return frames.tabs(@app, into: into)

# A new page of tabs `into`, at the end, whose tab reads `title`: a box that
# lays its children out one under another, as a column does, with a
# window's padding. The first page made is the one shown; a widget of a page
# not shown is out of the person's reach.
#
# - `app`: the app it is made in.
# - `into`: tabs.
# - `title`: its tab's text, one line without a zero byte.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `failed`. See also `tabs`.
function page(@app: App, into: Widget, title: str) -> Widget?
    return frames.page(@app, into: into, title: title)

# A new box in `into` whose content scrolls up and down, as a settings
# panel longer than its window, wxWidgets' `wxScrolledWindow`: it lays its
# children out one under another, as a column does, as wide as its inside
# and as high as they need, and the person scrolls it where they need more
# than it has. It needs its content's width, and its content's height up to
# 100 points; `set_fit`'s least height asks for more, `stretch` gives it the
# room left.
#
# - `app`: the app it is made in.
# - `into`: a window, a row, a column, a grid, a page, a scrolled box or a
#   pane.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `failed`.
function scrolled(@app: App, into: Widget) -> Widget?
    return frames.scrolled(@app, into: into)

# A new split in `into`: two panes and the divider between them, which the
# person drags, as a file manager's sidebar beside its files, wxWidgets'
# `wxSplitterWindow`. It needs its panes' needs and the divider's thickness
# along its line. The divider stands in the middle until the program
# (`set_value`) or the person moves it, always where neither pane gets less
# than it needs; `value_of` reads the first pane's width, or height, and the
# person dragging it is `.value_changed`.
#
# - `app`: the app it is made in.
# - `into`: a window, a row, a column, a grid, a page, a scrolled box or a
#   pane.
# - `side_by_side`: true for panes side by side, false for one over the
#   other.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `failed`. See also `pane`.
function split(@app: App, into: Widget, side_by_side: bool) -> Widget?
    return frames.split(@app, into: into, side_by_side: side_by_side)

# A new pane of split `into`, the first made the left or the top one: a box
# that lays its children out one under another, as a column does.
#
# - `app`: the app it is made in.
# - `into`: a split.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid` where the split holds two panes already,
# `failed`. See also `split`.
function pane(@app: App, into: Widget) -> Widget?
    return frames.pane(@app, into: into)

# Where a widget stands across its box's line when it does not fill it.
variant Align
    # At the left of a column's room, the top of a row's.
    start
    # In the middle.
    center
    # At the right of a column's room, the bottom of a row's.
    end

# How a widget, or a box, takes room in the box holding it. In a grid,
# `fill` and `align` hold on both axes of the cell.
record Fit
    # Its share of the room the box has left along its line, against its
    # siblings' weights; 0 keeps its least size. 0 to `MOST_STRETCH`.
    stretch: i64
    # Whether it takes the whole room across the box's line.
    fill: bool
    # Where it stands across the line when it does not fill.
    align: Align
    # The least width it takes, in points, whatever the system draws it
    # at; 0 to `MOST_SIZE`.
    min_width: i64
    # The least height it takes, in points; 0 to `MOST_SIZE`.
    min_height: i64
    # The room kept around it on every side, in points; 0 to `MOST_SIZE`.
    margin: i64

# The fit every widget starts with: no stretch, no fill, at the start, no
# least size of its own, no margin.
function default_fit() -> Fit
    return Fit(stretch: 0, fill: false, align: .start, min_width: 0, min_height: 0, margin: 0)

# Gives `widget` the fit `fit` in the box holding it.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: a widget or a box a window holds.
# - `fit`: how it takes room; every number within its field's range.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid` for a window, a menu, an item or a timer, or a
# number out of its range.
function set_fit(@app: App, widget: Widget, fit: Fit) -> ()?
    return state.set_fit(@app, widget: widget, fit: fit)

# Gives box or window `box` `spacing` points between two of its children and
# `padding` points inside its edges.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `box`: a window, a row, a column or a grid.
# - `spacing`: points, 0 to `MOST_SIZE`.
# - `padding`: points, 0 to `MOST_SIZE`.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`.
function set_spacing(@app: App, box: Widget, spacing: i64, padding: i64) -> ()?
    return state.set_spacing(@app, box: box, spacing: spacing, padding: padding)

# Gives grid `widget`'s columns and rows their weights for the room it has
# left, one weight a column and one a row, in order.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: the grid.
# - `columns`: a weight for each column from the first, 0 to
#   `MOST_STRETCH`; a column with none takes 0.
# - `rows`: a weight for each row from the first, likewise.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`.
function set_grid_stretch(@app: App, widget: Widget, columns: [i64], rows: [i64]) -> ()?
    return state.set_grid_stretch(@app, grid: widget, columns: columns, rows: rows)

## Widgets the person uses

# A new label in `into` showing `text`, a line feed starting a new line.
#
# - `app`: the app it is made in.
# - `into`: a window, a row, a column, a grid, a page, a scrolled box or a
#   pane.
# - `text`: what it shows, without a zero byte.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `limit`, `failed`.
function label(@app: App, into: Widget, text: str) -> Widget?
    return makers.make(@app, into: into, kind: hero_ui.HERO_UI_LABEL, text: text, items: [])

# A new button in `into` showing `text`; pressing it is `.clicked`.
#
# - `app`: the app it is made in.
# - `into`: a window, a row, a column, a grid, a page, a scrolled box or a
#   pane.
# - `text`: what it shows, one line without a zero byte.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `limit`, `failed`.
function button(@app: App, into: Widget, text: str) -> Widget?
    return makers.make(@app, into: into, kind: hero_ui.HERO_UI_BUTTON, text: text, items: [])

# The key that presses a button wherever its window has the keys.
variant ButtonKey
    # None, as a button is made.
    none
    # Return, the button drawn as its window's default, as a dialog's OK is.
    enter
    # Escape, as a dialog's Cancel is.
    escape

# Lets `key` press `widget` wherever its window has the keys, as wxWidgets'
# `wxButton::SetDefault` and a dialog's Cancel do; the button of its window
# that had that key loses it. Pressed so, it is `.clicked`. A window's
# button for Return takes Return before any of its text fields, as the
# systems' dialogs do.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: the button.
# - `key`: the key; `.none` takes it away.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid` for a widget no button, `failed`. See also
# `press_key`, `Event.submitted`.
function set_button_key(@app: App, widget: Widget, key: ButtonKey) -> ()?
    return state.set_button_key(@app, button: widget, key: key)

# A new text field in `into` holding `text`, one line the person types;
# each change is `.text_changed`.
#
# - `app`: the app it is made in.
# - `into`: a window, a row, a column, a grid, a page, a scrolled box or a
#   pane.
# - `text`: what it holds first, one line without a zero byte.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `limit`, `failed`.
function text_field(@app: App, into: Widget, text: str) -> Widget?
    return makers.make(@app, into: into, kind: hero_ui.HERO_UI_TEXT_FIELD, text: text, items: [])

# A new text field in `into` whose text the system masks as it is typed, a
# password's, as wxWidgets' `wxTE_PASSWORD`; it is a text field in every
# other way, read by `text_of` and told by `.text_changed` and
# `.submitted`. `render` draws one mark a character and never the text.
#
# - `app`: the app it is made in.
# - `into`: a window, a row, a column, a grid, a page, a scrolled box or a
#   pane.
# - `text`: what it holds first, one line without a zero byte.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `limit`, `failed`.
function password_field(@app: App, into: Widget, text: str) -> Widget?
    return makers.password_field(@app, into: into, text: text)

# A new text area in `into` holding `text`, lines the person types; each
# change is `.text_changed`.
#
# - `app`: the app it is made in.
# - `into`: a window, a row, a column, a grid, a page, a scrolled box or a
#   pane.
# - `text`: what it holds first, without a zero byte.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `limit`, `failed`.
function text_area(@app: App, into: Widget, text: str) -> Widget?
    return makers.make(@app, into: into, kind: hero_ui.HERO_UI_TEXT_AREA, text: text, items: [])

# A new checkbox in `into` showing `text`, checked or not; each click is
# `.toggled`.
#
# - `app`: the app it is made in.
# - `into`: a window, a row, a column, a grid, a page, a scrolled box or a
#   pane.
# - `text`: what it shows beside its box, one line without a zero byte.
# - `checked`: whether it starts checked.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `limit`, `failed`.
function checkbox(@app: App, into: Widget, text: str, checked: bool) -> Widget?
    return makers.checkbox(@app, into: into, text: text, checked: checked)

# A new radio group in `into` holding `items`, one under another, the first
# chosen; each choice is `.selection_changed`.
#
# - `app`: the app it is made in.
# - `into`: a window, a row, a column, a grid, a page, a scrolled box or a
#   pane.
# - `items`: the items, each one line without a zero byte; none makes a
#   group with nothing chosen.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `limit`, `failed`.
function radio_group(@app: App, into: Widget, items: [str]) -> Widget?
    return makers.make(@app, into: into, kind: hero_ui.HERO_UI_RADIO_GROUP, text: "", items: items)

# A new list in `into` holding `items`, nothing chosen; each change of what
# is chosen is `.selection_changed`, and a row opened by a double click or
# the Return key `.activated`.
#
# - `app`: the app it is made in.
# - `into`: a window, a row, a column, a grid, a page, a scrolled box or a
#   pane.
# - `items`: the items, each one line without a zero byte.
# - `multiple`: whether the person may choose several.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `limit`, `failed`.
function list(@app: App, into: Widget, items: [str], multiple: bool) -> Widget?
    kind = if multiple
        hero_ui.HERO_UI_LIST_MULTIPLE
    else
        hero_ui.HERO_UI_LIST

    return makers.make(@app, into: into, kind: kind, text: "", items: items)

# A new combo box in `into` holding `items`, the first chosen: a button
# showing the item chosen, which opens the others; each choice is
# `.selection_changed`.
#
# - `app`: the app it is made in.
# - `into`: a window, a row, a column, a grid, a page, a scrolled box or a
#   pane.
# - `items`: the items, each one line without a zero byte.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `limit`, `failed`.
function combo(@app: App, into: Widget, items: [str]) -> Widget?
    return makers.make(@app, into: into, kind: hero_ui.HERO_UI_COMBO, text: "", items: items)

# A new progress bar in `into`, empty, filled as its value goes from 0 to
# `most`.
#
# - `app`: the app it is made in.
# - `into`: a window, a row, a column, a grid, a page, a scrolled box or a
#   pane.
# - `most`: the value that fills it, 1 or more.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `limit`, `failed`. See also `set_value`.
function progress(@app: App, into: Widget, most: i64) -> Widget?
    return makers.ranged(@app, into: into, kind: hero_ui.HERO_UI_PROGRESS, least: 0, most: most, value: 0)

# Lets progress bar `widget` move with no value while the work's end is
# unknown, as wxWidgets' `wxGauge::Pulse`, or shows its value again.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: the progress bar.
# - `on`: true to let it move; false, as a bar is made, to show its value.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid` for a widget no progress bar, `failed`.
function set_moving(@app: App, widget: Widget, on: bool) -> ()?
    return state.set_moving(@app, widget: widget, on: on)

# A new slider in `into`, its knob at `value` between `least` and `most`;
# each move by the person is `.value_changed`.
#
# - `app`: the app it is made in.
# - `into`: a window, a row, a column, a grid, a page, a scrolled box or a
#   pane.
# - `least`: the value at its start, below `most`.
# - `most`: the value at its end.
# - `value`: where the knob starts, from `least` to `most`.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `limit`, `failed`.
function slider(@app: App, into: Widget, least: i64, most: i64, value: i64) -> Widget?
    return makers.ranged(@app, into: into, kind: hero_ui.HERO_UI_SLIDER, least: least, most: most, value: value)

# A new picture in `into` showing `img` at one point a pixel.
#
# - `app`: the app it is made in.
# - `into`: a window, a row, a column, a grid, a page, a scrolled box or a
#   pane.
# - `img`: the image, of any format, shown as 8-bit RGBA; its sides 0 to
#   `MOST_SIZE`.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid` where `img` is not valid, `limit`, `failed`.
function picture(@app: App, into: Widget, img: image.Image) -> Widget?
    return makers.picture(@app, into: into, img: img)

## What a widget shows

# Sets what `widget` shows as its text: a window's title, a label's, a
# button's, a checkbox's, a menu's or a menu item's text, or the whole text
# of a text field or a text area. No event comes of it.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: one of the kinds above.
# - `text`: without a zero byte, one line for all but a label and a text
#   area.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `failed`.
function set_text(@app: App, widget: Widget, text: str) -> ()?
    return state.set_text(@app, widget: widget, text: text)

# The text `widget` shows: for a text field or a text area, what the person
# has typed into it as it stands now.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: one of the kinds `set_text` sets.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `failed`.
function text_of(app: App, widget: Widget) -> str?
    return state.text_of(app, widget: widget)

# Lets the person use `widget`, or greys it out; a widget greyed out gives
# no event.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: any widget but a window, a box or a timer.
# - `on`: true to let the person use it, as a new widget does.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `failed`.
function set_enabled(@app: App, widget: Widget, on: bool) -> ()?
    return state.set_enabled(@app, widget: widget, on: on)

# Shows or hides `widget`; a hidden widget or box takes no room, and what
# it holds is hidden with it.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: a window, or a widget or a box a window holds.
# - `on`: true to show it, as a new widget is.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid` for a menu, an item or a timer, `failed`.
function set_visible(@app: App, widget: Widget, on: bool) -> ()?
    return state.set_visible(@app, widget: widget, on: on)

# Checks or unchecks checkbox `widget`, or a check or radio item of a menu;
# a radio item checked unchecks the others of its group. No event comes of
# it.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: the checkbox, or the menu's check or radio item.
# - `checked`: whether it is checked.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid` for a widget of another kind or a plain menu
# item, `failed`. See also `check_item`, `radio_item`.
function set_checked(@app: App, widget: Widget, checked: bool) -> ()?
    return state.set_checked(@app, widget: widget, checked: checked)

# Whether checkbox `widget`, or a check or radio item of a menu, is checked
# now, the person's last choice included.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: the checkbox, or the menu's check or radio item.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid` for a widget of another kind or a plain menu
# item, `failed`.
function is_checked(app: App, widget: Widget) -> bool?
    return state.is_checked(app, widget: widget)

# Moves a slider's knob, or fills a progress bar, to `value`, or puts a
# split's divider `value` points from its start, kept where neither pane
# gets less than it needs. No event comes of it.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: a slider, a progress bar or a split.
# - `value`: from its lowest value to its highest; for a split 0 to
#   `MOST_SIZE`.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `failed`. See also `set_range`.
function set_value(@app: App, widget: Widget, value: i64) -> ()?
    return state.set_value(@app, widget: widget, value: value, kind: hero_ui.HERO_UI_SLIDER)

# The value a slider's knob or a progress bar stands at now, or the place a
# split's divider stands at, the first pane's width or height.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: a slider, a progress bar or a split.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `failed`.
function value_of(app: App, widget: Widget) -> i64?
    return state.value_of(app, widget: widget, kind: hero_ui.HERO_UI_SLIDER)

# Makes slider or progress bar `widget` run from `least` to `most`, its
# value moved inside where it falls outside.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: a slider or a progress bar.
# - `least`: its lowest value, below `most`; 0 for a progress bar.
# - `most`: its highest value.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `failed`.
function set_range(@app: App, widget: Widget, least: i64, most: i64) -> ()?
    return state.set_range(@app, widget: widget, least: least, most: most)

# Gives a list, a combo box or a radio group the items `items`: a list then
# has nothing chosen, a combo box and a radio group their first. No event
# comes of it.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: a list, a combo box or a radio group.
# - `items`: each one line without a zero byte.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `failed`.
function set_items(@app: App, widget: Widget, items: [str]) -> ()?
    return state.set_items(@app, widget: widget, items: items)

# Chooses the items at `indices` of a list, a combo box or a radio group,
# or the rows of a table, or shows the page of tabs at its one index, or
# chooses a tree's item by its number, the items over it unfolded. No
# event comes of it.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: a list, a combo box, a radio group, tabs, a table or a tree.
# - `indices`: item or row indices, ascending and each once, at most one
#   but for a list of several choices and a table; none chooses nothing;
#   exactly one for tabs; a tree's item number, or none.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid` where an index is past the items or the indices
# are out of order, `failed`.
function set_selection(@app: App, widget: Widget, indices: [i64]) -> ()?
    return state.set_selection(@app, widget: widget, indices: indices)

# The indices of the items a list, a combo box or a radio group has chosen
# now, or the rows of a table, ascending, empty where none is; the index of
# the page tabs show; the number of the item a tree has chosen.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: a list, a combo box, a radio group, tabs, a table or a tree.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `failed`.
function selection_of(app: App, widget: Widget) -> [i64]?
    return state.selection_of(app, widget: widget)

# Shows `img` in picture `widget`, which takes its size.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: a picture.
# - `img`: the image, of any format; its sides 0 to `MOST_SIZE`.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `failed`.
function set_picture(@app: App, widget: Widget, img: image.Image) -> ()?
    return state.set_picture(@app, widget: widget, img: img)

## Menus

# A new menu titled `title` at the end of window `into`'s menu bar, or at
# the end of menu or context menu `into`, a menu inside a menu (Open
# Recent, Format > Font). On macOS the menu bar at the top of the screen
# shows the menus of the window in front, after the application's own menu.
#
# - `app`: the app it is made in.
# - `into`: the window, the menu or the context menu.
# - `title`: one line without a zero byte.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `limit`, `failed`.
function menu(@app: App, into: Widget, title: str) -> Widget?
    return makers.menu_part(@app, into: into, kind: hero_ui.HERO_UI_MENU, text: title)

# A new context menu of `widget`: the menu the system shows where the
# person right-clicks the widget, or Control-clicks it on macOS, as a
# file's Open, Rename and Delete, wxWidgets' `wxWindow::PopupMenu`. Its
# items are made by `menu_item`, `menu_separator`, `check_item`,
# `radio_item` and `menu` into it, and choosing one is `.menu_chosen`; a
# shortcut set on one of its items is shown and takes no key. One a widget;
# `destroy` takes it away.
#
# - `app`: the app it is made in.
# - `widget`: a widget the person clicks, or a window for its content.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid` for a menu, a menu's item, a timer, a page or a
# pane, or a widget with a context menu already, `failed`.
function context_menu(@app: App, widget: Widget) -> Widget?
    return makers.context_menu(@app, widget: widget)

# A new item at the end of menu or context menu `into`, showing `text`;
# choosing it, by the mouse or its shortcut, is `.menu_chosen`.
#
# - `app`: the app it is made in.
# - `into`: the menu or the context menu.
# - `text`: one line without a zero byte.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `limit`, `failed`. See also `set_shortcut`.
function menu_item(@app: App, into: Widget, text: str) -> Widget?
    return makers.menu_part(@app, into: into, kind: hero_ui.HERO_UI_MENU_ITEM, text: text)

# A new line at the end of menu `into`, between the items before and after.
#
# - `app`: the app it is made in.
# - `into`: the menu.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `limit`, `failed`.
function menu_separator(@app: App, into: Widget) -> Widget?
    return makers.menu_part(@app, into: into, kind: hero_ui.HERO_UI_MENU_SEPARATOR, text: "")

# A new item at the end of menu or context menu `into` that the person
# checks and unchecks by choosing it (View > Show Sidebar), as wxWidgets'
# `wxITEM_CHECK`; each choice turns it over before its `.menu_chosen`.
#
# - `app`: the app it is made in.
# - `into`: the menu or the context menu.
# - `text`: one line without a zero byte.
# - `checked`: whether it starts checked.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `limit`, `failed`. See also `is_checked`,
# `set_checked`.
function check_item(@app: App, into: Widget, text: str, checked: bool) -> Widget?
    return menus.checkable(@app, into: into, text: text, mode: hero_ui.HERO_UI_CHECK_BOX, checked: checked)

# A new item at the end of menu or context menu `into` of a group of which
# one is checked (View > as Icons, as List), as wxWidgets' `wxITEM_RADIO`:
# the radio items of a menu no other item or separator stands between are
# one group, and choosing one checks it and unchecks the others before its
# `.menu_chosen`.
#
# - `app`: the app it is made in.
# - `into`: the menu or the context menu.
# - `text`: one line without a zero byte.
# - `checked`: whether it starts checked, the others of its group then
#   unchecked.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `limit`, `failed`. See also `is_checked`,
# `set_checked`.
function radio_item(@app: App, into: Widget, text: str, checked: bool) -> Widget?
    return menus.checkable(@app, into: into, text: text, mode: hero_ui.HERO_UI_CHECK_RADIO, checked: checked)

# A key and the keys held with it, which choose a menu item.
record Shortcut
    # The key: one letter `a` to `z`, one digit, or one of `F1` to `F12`,
    # `Enter`, `Escape`, `Tab`, `Space`, `Delete`, `Backspace`, `Up`,
    # `Down`, `Left`, `Right`, `Home`, `End`, `PageUp`, `PageDown`; empty
    # for no shortcut.
    key: str
    # Whether the system's own key for shortcuts is held: Command on macOS,
    # Control elsewhere.
    primary: bool
    # Whether Shift is held.
    shift: bool
    # Whether Alt is held, Option on macOS.
    alt: bool

# Gives menu item `item` the shortcut `shortcut`, or takes it away with an
# empty key.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `item`: the menu item.
# - `shortcut`: its key, one the record names.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid` for a key the record does not name, `failed`.
function set_shortcut(@app: App, item: Widget, shortcut: Shortcut) -> ()?
    return state.set_shortcut(@app, item: item, shortcut: shortcut)

# What a menu item stands for among what every program of the system has,
# in the manner of wxWidgets' `wxID_ABOUT`, `wxID_PREFERENCES` and
# `wxID_EXIT`.
variant MenuRole
    # Nothing but itself, as a new item.
    plain
    # The program's About box.
    about
    # The program's settings.
    settings
    # Quitting the program.
    quit

# Gives menu item `item` the role `role`. On macOS an item with a role stands
# in the application's menu, in the place every Mac program has it (About
# first, Settings with Command+, where it has no shortcut of its own, Quit
# last), and is hidden in the menu it was made in; elsewhere it stays where
# it was made. Choosing it is `.menu_chosen` naming it on every system. The
# application's menu of macOS holds the system's Services, Hide, Hide Others
# and Show All whatever the program gives; without an item of the role
# `about` it shows the system's own About panel, and without one of `quit`
# its Quit asks every window to close.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `item`: the menu item.
# - `role`: its role; `.plain` takes one away.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid` for a widget no menu item, `failed`. See also
# `menu_item`, `set_shortcut`.
function set_role(@app: App, item: Widget, role: MenuRole) -> ()?
    return state.set_role(@app, item: item, role: role)

## Tables

# How a table shows itself sorted.
variant Sort
    # By no column.
    unsorted
    # By one column.
    by
        # The column, from 0.
        column: i64
        # Whether ascending.
        ascending: bool

# A new table in `into`: rows of cells under the headers `columns`, which
# the person drags to widen and clicks to ask for the rows sorted, several
# rows chosen at once, as a file manager's details or a mail client's
# messages, wxWidgets' `wxDataViewListCtrl`. It holds no row until
# `set_rows`; `set_selection` and `selection_of` choose and read rows; the
# person choosing rows is `.selection_changed`, opening one by a double
# click or Return `.activated`, clicking a header `.sort_asked`.
#
# - `app`: the app it is made in.
# - `into`: a window, a row, a column, a grid, a page, a scrolled box or a
#   pane.
# - `columns`: the headers' titles, 1 to 64, each one line without a zero
#   byte.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `failed`. See also `set_rows`, `set_sort`.
function table(@app: App, into: Widget, columns: [str]) -> Widget?
    return tables.table(@app, into: into, columns: columns)

# Gives table `widget` the rows `rows`, in the order they are shown, none
# chosen. No event comes of it. A program sorting its rows on
# `.sort_asked` gives them here again in their new order.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: a table.
# - `rows`: each a cell for every column, one line without a zero byte.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid` for a row of another count of cells or a cell
# of two lines, `failed`. See also `rows_of`.
function set_rows(@app: App, widget: Widget, rows: [[str]]) -> ()?
    return tables.set_rows(@app, widget: widget, rows: rows)

# The rows table `widget` holds, in the order shown.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: a table.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`.
function rows_of(app: App, widget: Widget) -> [[str]]?
    return tables.rows_of(app, widget: widget)

# Shows table `widget` sorted as `order` says, its column's header showing
# the arrow. No event comes of it, and the rows stand as they are.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: a table.
# - `order`: `.unsorted`, or `.by` one of its columns.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `failed`. See also `.sort_asked`.
function set_sort(@app: App, widget: Widget, order: Sort) -> ()?
    return tables.set_sort(@app, widget: widget, order: order)

# How table `widget` shows itself sorted: as the program set it or the
# person's click left it.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: a table.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`.
function sort_of(app: App, widget: Widget) -> Sort?
    return tables.sort_of(app, widget: widget)

## Trees

# A new tree in `into`: items under items, each folded and unfolded by the
# person, one chosen, as a disk's folders or a mail client's mailboxes,
# wxWidgets' `wxTreeCtrl`. It holds no item until `add_item`;
# `set_selection` and `selection_of` choose and read the item chosen by its
# number; the person choosing one is `.selection_changed`, opening one by a
# double click or Return `.activated` with its number, unfolding or folding
# one `.unfolded`.
#
# - `app`: the app it is made in.
# - `into`: a window, a row, a column, a grid, a page, a scrolled box or a
#   pane.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `failed`. See also `add_item`.
function tree(@app: App, into: Widget) -> Widget?
    return trees.tree(@app, into: into)

# Adds an item showing `text` at the end of the items under item `under`
# of tree `widget`, and answers its number, the tree's next from 1. A
# foldable item shows its triangle before it holds an item, so the program
# adds what is under it on `.unfolded`, as a disk's folders load.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: a tree.
# - `under`: the item it stands under; 0 for the top.
# - `text`: one line without a zero byte.
# - `foldable`: true where the person may unfold it before it holds an
#   item.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `failed`. See also `remove_item`.
function add_item(@app: App, widget: Widget, under: i64, text: str, foldable: bool) -> i64?
    return trees.add(@app, widget: widget, under: under, text: text, foldable: foldable)

# Takes item `item` out of tree `widget`, with every item under it; the
# choice of one of them goes with it. No event comes of it.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: a tree.
# - `item`: the item's number.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `failed`.
function remove_item(@app: App, widget: Widget, item: i64) -> ()?
    return trees.remove(@app, widget: widget, item: item)

# The numbers of the items right under item `item` of tree `widget`, in
# order.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: a tree.
# - `item`: the item's number; 0 for the top.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`.
function items_under(app: App, widget: Widget, item: i64) -> [i64]?
    return trees.under_of(app, widget: widget, item: item)

# The text item `item` of tree `widget` shows.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: a tree.
# - `item`: the item's number.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`.
function item_text(app: App, widget: Widget, item: i64) -> str?
    return trees.text_of(app, widget: widget, item: item)

# Unfolds or folds item `item` of tree `widget`. No event comes of it.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: a tree.
# - `item`: a foldable item, or one holding items.
# - `on`: true to unfold it, false to fold it.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `failed`.
function set_unfolded(@app: App, widget: Widget, item: i64, on: bool) -> ()?
    return trees.set_unfolded(@app, widget: widget, item: item, on: on)

# Whether item `item` of tree `widget` is unfolded.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: a tree.
# - `item`: the item's number.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`.
function is_unfolded(app: App, widget: Widget, item: i64) -> bool?
    return trees.is_unfolded(app, widget: widget, item: item)

## The clipboard

# What the system's clipboard holds of one kind, or takes in place of all
# it held, as wxWidgets' `wxTextDataObject`, `wxFileDataObject` and
# `wxBitmapDataObject`.
variant Clip
    # Nothing: a read found nothing of the kind it asked for, and a write
    # empties the clipboard.
    nothing
    # Text.
    text
        # The text, holding no zero byte.
        text: str
    # Files and folders, as a file manager copies them.
    files
        # Their absolute paths, at least one.
        paths: [str]
    # An image, as a screenshot is copied.
    picture
        # The image; a read answers 8-bit RGBA.
        img: image.Image

# The kind a read of the clipboard asks for.
variant ClipKind
    # Text.
    text
    # Files and folders.
    files
    # An image.
    picture

# The most bytes the clipboard takes or answers: an image of 8192 by 8192
# pixels, at 4 bytes a pixel.
constant CLIPBOARD_MOST: i64
    hero_ui.HERO_UI_CLIP_MOST

# Puts `clip` on the clipboard in place of all it held, as a program's Copy
# does: a list's paths copied as text, a file manager's files, a picture.
# `.nothing` empties it, as a password manager does once the password is
# pasted. On the native side it is the system's clipboard, which every
# program pastes from; on the headless side the app's own, which only the
# program writes. A text field's Cut, Copy and Paste need none of this:
# the Edit menu reaches the clipboard for them.
#
# - `app`: the app.
# - `clip`: what it holds next: text holding no zero byte; paths each
#   absolute, none empty or holding a zero byte; or a valid image of at
#   least one pixel, at most `CLIPBOARD_MOST` bytes in 8-bit RGBA.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`, `limit` past `CLIPBOARD_MOST` bytes, `failed`.
# See also `clipboard`.
function set_clipboard(@app: App, clip: Clip) -> ()?
    return clips.put(@app, clip: clip)

# What the clipboard holds as `kind`, as a program's Paste reads it, or
# `.nothing` where it holds nothing of that kind. On the native side another
# program may have written it, offering several kinds at once, and a read
# finds the kind asked where that program offered it; on the headless side
# a read answers only what `set_clipboard` put. On both, what the program
# puts is found as its own kind alone.
#
# - `app`: the app.
# - `kind`: the kind asked for.
#
# Fails `closed`, `limit` where it holds past `CLIPBOARD_MOST` bytes,
# `failed`. See also `set_clipboard`.
function clipboard(app: App, kind: ClipKind) -> Clip?
    return clips.read(app, kind: kind)

## Timers

# A new timer of `app` that fires every `every`, as `.timer_fired`, the
# first time `every` from now, until destroyed. A timer that fell behind
# fires once and counts its next period from then.
#
# - `app`: the app whose `wait` it fires in.
# - `every`: its period, from a millisecond to a day.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid`. See also `destroy`.
function timer(@app: App, every: time.Duration) -> Widget?
    return makers.timer(@app, every: every)

## Events

# What the person did, as `wait` answers it, naming the widget by the
# handle the program holds. Nothing the program sets is answered.
variant Event
    # A button was pressed.
    clicked
        # The button.
        widget: Widget
    # The person changed the text of a text field or a text area.
    text_changed
        # The text field or the text area.
        widget: Widget
        # Its whole text now.
        text: str
    # The person changed what a list, a combo box or a radio group has
    # chosen.
    selection_changed
        # The list, the combo box or the radio group.
        widget: Widget
        # The indices chosen now, ascending; empty where none is.
        indices: [i64]
    # The person checked or unchecked a checkbox.
    toggled
        # The checkbox.
        widget: Widget
        # Whether it is checked now.
        checked: bool
    # The person moved a slider's knob.
    value_changed
        # The slider.
        widget: Widget
        # Its value now.
        value: i64
    # The person chose a menu item, by the mouse or its shortcut.
    menu_chosen
        # The menu item.
        item: Widget
    # The person asked a window to close, by its close button or the
    # system's quit. Nothing is closed: the program destroys the window, or
    # keeps it.
    close_asked
        # The window.
        window: Widget
    # A timer's period passed.
    timer_fired
        # The timer.
        timer: Widget
    # A window's content took a new size; its widgets are laid out again
    # already.
    resized
        # The window.
        window: Widget
        # Its content's width now, in points.
        width: i64
        # Its content's height now, in points.
        height: i64
    # The person moved a window, by its title bar or a key of the system's.
    moved
        # The window.
        window: Widget
        # The screen it stands on now, from 0.
        screen: i64
        # Its frame's left edge from that screen's left edge, in points.
        x: i64
        # Its frame's top edge from that screen's top edge, in points.
        y: i64
    # The person brought a window to another state, by its buttons, the
    # Dock or the task bar, or a key of the system's; the size its content
    # took comes as `.resized`.
    state_changed
        # The window.
        window: Widget
        # Its state now.
        state: WindowState
    # A window stands on another screen, moved there by the person or left
    # there by a screen that changed; its `.moved` comes after.
    screen_changed
        # The window.
        window: Widget
        # The screen, from 0.
        screen: i64
    # The person pressed Return in a text field: a search field searches, a
    # chat field sends; its text is the field's (`text_of`). A window whose
    # button Return presses (`set_button_key`) gives that button's
    # `.clicked` instead.
    submitted
        # The text field.
        widget: Widget
    # The person opened a row of a list, by a double click on it or by the
    # Return key while the list had the keys: a file manager enters the
    # folder, a mail client opens the message. A first click's choice comes
    # before it as `.selection_changed`.
    activated
        # The list or the table.
        widget: Widget
        # The row opened, from 0; for the Return key, the first row chosen.
        index: i64
    # The person clicked a table's column header, asking for its rows
    # sorted by that column: the column sorted by already turns over,
    # another starts ascending, and its arrow shows it at once
    # (`set_sort`). The program sorts its rows and gives them again.
    sort_asked
        # The table.
        widget: Widget
        # The column, from 0.
        column: i64
        # Whether ascending.
        ascending: bool
    # The person unfolded or folded an item of a tree: an item unfolded the
    # first time while it holds none is the program's moment to add what is
    # under it (`add_item`).
    unfolded
        # The tree.
        widget: Widget
        # The item's number.
        item: i64
        # True for unfolded, false for folded.
        open: bool
    # A web view's page sent a text by its bridge, `heroes.send` (the
    # framing of `ui/webview`'s `parse_call` reads a call in it).
    message
        # The web view.
        widget: Widget
        # What the page sent.
        text: str

# The next thing the person did, waiting at most `within` for it: windows
# made or changed are laid out and shown first, then the inputs `inject`
# queued are given one at a time, then the system's own events and the
# timers come in the order they happened.
#
# - `app`: the app waited on.
# - `within`: the most time to wait, 0 to answer only what is ready; a
#   headless app's clock moves by it, a native app waits it.
#
# Fails `timeout` where nothing came in `within`; `closed` where `app` is,
# or where a headless app's injected inputs are spent and no timer runs, so
# that a demo's loop ends where its inputs do; `invalid` for a time below
# zero; `failed`.
function wait(@app: App, within: time.Duration) -> Event?
    return loop.wait(@app, within: within)

## Input from the program

# What a person does, given by the program: to drive a headless app, whose
# person is the program itself, or a native one in a test.
variant Input
    # Presses a button.
    click
        # The button.
        widget: Widget
    # Replaces the whole text of a text field or a text area, as typing
    # would.
    type_text
        # The text field or the text area.
        widget: Widget
        # Its text after it, one line for a text field.
        text: str
    # Chooses items of a list, a combo box or a radio group, as clicks would.
    choose
        # The list, the combo box or the radio group.
        widget: Widget
        # The indices, ascending and each once: exactly one for a combo box
        # or a radio group, at most one for a list of one choice.
        indices: [i64]
    # Clicks a checkbox.
    toggle
        # The checkbox.
        widget: Widget
    # Drags a slider's knob to a value.
    slide
        # The slider.
        widget: Widget
        # The value, from its lowest to its highest.
        value: i64
    # Chooses a menu item.
    choose_menu
        # The menu item.
        item: Widget
    # Presses a window's close button.
    ask_close
        # The window.
        window: Widget
    # Drags a window's edge so its content is a new size.
    resize
        # The window.
        window: Widget
        # The width asked, 0 to `MOST_SIZE`; the window keeps what it needs.
        width: i64
        # The height asked, 0 to `MOST_SIZE`.
        height: i64
    # Drags a window by its title bar to a position on a screen.
    drag_window
        # The window.
        window: Widget
        # The screen, a screen of `screens`.
        screen: i64
        # Its frame's left edge from that screen's left edge, in points,
        # `-MOST_SIZE` to `MOST_SIZE`.
        x: i64
        # Its frame's top edge from that screen's top edge, the same range.
        y: i64
    # Brings a window to a state, as its buttons or the system's keys would.
    change_state
        # The window.
        window: Widget
        # The state.
        state: WindowState
    # Presses Return in a text field.
    submit
        # The text field.
        widget: Widget
    # Opens a row of a list or a table as a double click on it does,
    # choosing it first where it is not chosen.
    activate
        # The list or the table.
        widget: Widget
        # The row, an index of its items or rows.
        index: i64
    # Clicks a table's column header until the table stands sorted by it in
    # the order asked.
    sort
        # The table.
        widget: Widget
        # The column, from 0.
        column: i64
        # Whether ascending.
        ascending: bool
    # Unfolds or folds an item of a tree the person sees, by its triangle.
    unfold
        # The tree.
        widget: Widget
        # The item's number.
        item: i64
        # True to unfold, false to fold.
        open: bool
    # Presses a button of the next message box `message` shows.
    answer
        # The button.
        answer: Answer
    # Chooses files in the next file dialog `open_files` or `save_file`
    # shows.
    pick_files
        # The paths chosen, one or more; exactly one for `save_file`.
        paths: [str]
    # Cancels the next file dialog.
    cancel_files
    # Has a web view's page send a text by its bridge, as its own script's
    # `heroes.send` would: given once the web view shows a page of the
    # app's own origin, which on the headless side is once `ui/webview`'s
    # `navigate` named one.
    send
        # The web view.
        widget: Widget
        # The text, without a zero byte.
        text: str

# Queues `input`, what a person does: the next `wait` that has no event
# ready gives it, through the system's own path on the native side, so its
# event comes as the person's would; an answer or a file dialog's choice
# waits for the next dialog instead. An input on a widget greyed out,
# hidden, or destroyed before it is given, gives no event, as a person's
# could not.
#
# - `app`: the app it is given to.
# - `input`: the input, checked now against the widget as it stands.
#
# Fails `closed` where `app` or the widget is, `invalid` where the widget is
# of another kind than the input acts on or a value is outside its range.
function inject(@app: App, input: Input) -> ()?
    return loop.inject(@app, input: input)

# Presses a key now, as the person's keyboard would, with `widget` holding
# the keys, given them first as the person's click would; the events it
# makes come at the next `wait`s. On the native side the key goes through
# the system's own path, so the menu item whose shortcut it is, the
# system's Edit menu (undo, redo, cut, copy, paste and select all in a text
# widget) and the widget take it as the person's; on the headless side,
# whose widgets no system edits, Return and Escape press the window's button
# for them (`set_button_key`), a key chooses the first item of the window's
# menus whose shortcut it is, the item and its menu enabled, as
# `.menu_chosen`, and Return in a text field is `.submitted`; nothing else.
# A widget greyed out or hidden takes no key, as a person could not give it
# one.
#
# - `app`: the app it is pressed in; a native one's windows are laid out and
#   shown first.
# - `widget`: a window, or a text field, a text area or a list of one.
# - `shortcut`: the key and the keys held with it, as a menu item's
#   shortcut names them; not an empty key.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid` for an empty key, one no shortcut names or a
# widget of another kind; `failed` where the system keeps another
# application in front, so no window of this app can take a key. See also
# `set_shortcut`, `inject`.
function press_key(@app: App, widget: Widget, shortcut: Shortcut) -> ()?
    return keyboard.press(@app, widget: widget, shortcut: shortcut)

# Gives `widget` the keys in its window, as wxWidgets' `wxWindow::SetFocus`:
# what the person types goes to it once its window is in front. Tab and
# Shift+Tab move the keys through the widgets in the order they stand, left
# to right and top to bottom, as the layout reads them.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: a text field, a text area or a list of a window, shown and not
#   greyed out.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid` for a widget of another kind, hidden or greyed
# out, `failed`. See also `focus_of`, `press_key`.
function set_focus(@app: App, widget: Widget) -> ()?
    return keyboard.set_focus(@app, widget: widget)

# The widget of window `widget` that has the keys: what the person types
# goes to it while the window is in front; the window itself where none of
# its widgets has them.
#
# - `app`: the app holding it.
# - `widget`: the window.
#
# Fails `closed`, `invalid` for a widget no window, `failed`. See also
# `set_focus`.
function focus_of(app: App, widget: Widget) -> Widget?
    return keyboard.focus_of(app, window: widget)

## Dialogs

# The tone of a message box, which its icon shows.
variant Tone
    # A fact the person is told.
    info
    # Something to think about before going on.
    warning
    # Something that failed.
    error

# The buttons of a message box.
variant Buttons
    # OK alone.
    ok
    # OK and Cancel.
    ok_cancel
    # Yes and No.
    yes_no
    # Yes, No and Cancel.
    yes_no_cancel

# The button the person pressed in a message box.
variant Answer
    # OK.
    ok
    # Cancel, or the box closed by Escape.
    cancel
    # Yes.
    yes
    # No.
    no
    # A button of a box of the program's words (`ask`).
    button
        # Its index among the box's buttons, from 0.
        index: i64

# Files a file dialog offers, by their extensions.
record Filter
    # What the person reads: `Images`.
    name: str
    # The extensions without their dot, `png`, `jpg`; none for every file.
    extensions: [str]

# What the person did in a file dialog.
variant Choice
    # They cancelled it.
    cancelled
    # They chose files.
    chosen
        # The paths, one or more; one for a save.
        paths: [str]

# Shows a message box over window `over` and waits for the person to press
# one of its buttons; an answer `inject` queued is taken instead, and no box
# is shown.
#
# - `app`: the app showing it.
# - `over`: the window it stands over.
# - `title`: its title, one line without a zero byte.
# - `text`: what it says, without a zero byte.
# - `tone`: its tone.
# - `buttons`: its buttons.
#
# Fails `closed`; `invalid` where an injected answer is no button of the
# box; `closed` on a headless app where no answer was injected, since no
# person will ever answer; `failed`.
function message(@app: App, over: Widget, title: str, text: str, tone: Tone, buttons: Buttons) -> Answer?
    return dialogs.message(@app, over: over, title: title, text: text, tone: tone, buttons: buttons)

# Shows the system's dialog to open files over window `over` and waits for
# the person; a choice `inject` queued is taken instead, and no dialog is
# shown.
#
# - `app`: the app showing it.
# - `over`: the window it stands over.
# - `title`: its title, one line without a zero byte.
# - `filters`: the files offered; none offers every file.
# - `multiple`: whether the person may choose several.
#
# Fails `closed`, and `closed` on a headless app where no choice was
# injected; `invalid` where an injected choice holds several paths and
# `multiple` is false; `failed`.
function open_files(@app: App, over: Widget, title: str, filters: [Filter], multiple: bool) -> Choice?
    return dialogs.files(@app, over: over, title: title, name: "", filters: filters, save: false, multiple: multiple)

# Shows the system's dialog to save a file over window `over`, proposing the
# name `name`, and waits for the person; a choice `inject` queued is taken
# instead.
#
# - `app`: the app showing it.
# - `over`: the window it stands over.
# - `title`: its title, one line without a zero byte.
# - `name`: the file name proposed, without a zero byte.
# - `filters`: the files offered; none offers every file.
#
# Fails `closed`, and `closed` on a headless app where no choice was
# injected; `invalid` where an injected choice holds more than one path;
# `failed`.
function save_file(@app: App, over: Widget, title: str, name: str, filters: [Filter]) -> Choice?
    return dialogs.files(@app, over: over, title: title, name: name, filters: filters, save: true, multiple: false)

# Shows the system's dialog to choose a folder over window `over` and waits
# for the person, as wxWidgets' `wxDirDialog`; a choice `inject` queued is
# taken instead, and no dialog is shown.
#
# - `app`: the app showing it.
# - `over`: the window it stands over.
# - `title`: its title, one line without a zero byte.
# - `multiple`: whether the person may choose several folders.
#
# The paths are the folders'. Fails `closed`, and `closed` on a headless app
# where no choice was injected; `invalid` where an injected choice holds
# several paths and `multiple` is false; `failed`. See also `open_files`.
function open_folders(@app: App, over: Widget, title: str, multiple: bool) -> Choice?
    return dialogs.folders(@app, over: over, title: title, multiple: multiple)

# Shows a message box over window `over` whose buttons say the program's
# own words, as "Save", "Don't Save" and "Cancel" before a document closes,
# and waits for the person to press one; answers its index. An answer
# `inject` queued is taken instead and no box is shown: `.button(index:)`,
# or `.cancel` for the button Escape presses.
#
# - `app`: the app showing it.
# - `over`: the window it stands over.
# - `title`: its title, one line without a zero byte.
# - `text`: what it says, without a zero byte.
# - `tone`: its tone.
# - `buttons`: the buttons' labels in the order the person reads them, 1
#   to 16, each one line, not empty, without a zero byte; the first is
#   pressed by Return.
# - `cancel`: the index of the button Escape presses, or -1 for none.
#
# Fails `closed`; `invalid` for a label or a `cancel` out of its range, or
# an injected answer that is no button of the box; `closed` on a headless
# app where no answer was injected; `failed`. See also `message`.
function ask(@app: App, over: Widget, title: str, text: str, tone: Tone, buttons: [str], cancel: i64) -> i64?
    return dialogs.ask(@app, over: over, title: title, text: text, tone: tone, buttons: buttons, cancel: cancel)

## Drawing as text

# Every window of `app` as text, laid out first where anything changed: a
# line for each window, then each widget it holds, two spaces deeper for
# each box or menu around it, in the order made. A line names the kind and
# the number, then what it shows and its state, then `at X,Y WxH`
# where the layout placed it; a window says its title and its content's
# size, a hidden widget `hidden` and no place, a greyed-out one `disabled`.
# A window that stands otherwise than a new one says so after its size:
# `on screen S at X,Y` once the program or the person placed it, then
# `minimized`, `maximized` or `full_screen`, `on_top`, `frameless`,
# `opacity N`, `translucent` and `modal`. Texts are quoted, a quote, a backslash, a line feed and a tab escaped. The
# same app draws the same lines on every machine and on both sides.
#
# On the headless side each widget takes the size `ui/internal/sizes` gives
# its kind: a column of text 8 points wide, a line of a label 16 high, a
# button its text and 24 around it by 24, a text field 160 by 24, a text
# area 240 by 96, a checkbox its text and 24 by 20, a radio group its
# widest item and 24 by 20 an item, a list 160 by 96, a combo box its widest
# item and 40, at least 80, by 24, a progress bar 160 by 16, a slider 160
# by 24, a picture its pixels.
#
# - `app`: the app drawn; its windows are laid out where needed.
#
# Fails `closed` where `app` is.
function render(@app: App) -> [str]?
    return drawing.lines(@app)

## Tests

test "render: every kind of widget in one window, drawn as text"
    app @= fixture.test_app()
    w = fixture.window_of(app)
    m = menu(@app, into: w, title: "File").must()
    open_item = menu_item(@app, into: m, text: "Open").must()
    set_shortcut(@app, item: open_item, shortcut: Shortcut(key: "o", primary: true, shift: false, alt: false)).must()
    _ = menu_separator(@app, into: m).must()
    r = row(@app, into: w).must()
    _ = label(@app, into: r, text: "Name").must()
    _ = text_field(@app, into: r, text: "Ada").must()
    _ = text_area(@app, into: w, text: "one\ntwo \"quoted\"").must()
    g = grid(@app, into: w, columns: 2).must()
    _ = checkbox(@app, into: g, text: "Wrap", checked: true).must()
    _ = radio_group(@app, into: g, items: ["S", "M"]).must()
    _ = list(@app, into: g, items: ["a", "b"], multiple: true).must()
    _ = combo(@app, into: g, items: ["x"]).must()
    p = progress(@app, into: w, most: 10).must()
    set_value(@app, widget: p, value: 4).must()
    _ = slider(@app, into: w, least: -5, most: 5, value: 1).must()
    _ = picture(@app, into: w, img: image.blank(width: 3, height: 2, format: .rgba).must()).must()
    b = button(@app, into: w, text: "Go").must()
    set_enabled(@app, widget: b, on: false).must()
    lines = render(@app).must()
    assert lines == ["window #1 \"Test\" 300x388", "  menu #2 \"File\"", "    menu_item #3 \"Open\" primary+o", "    menu_separator #4", "  row #5 at 12,12 198x24", "    label #6 \"Name\" at 12,12 32x16", "    text_field #7 \"Ada\" at 50,12 160x24", "  text_area #8 \"one\\ntwo \\\"quoted\\\"\" at 12,42 240x96", "  grid #9 2 columns at 12,144 246x142", "    checkbox #10 [x] \"Wrap\" at 12,144 56x20", "    radio_group #11 [\"S\", \"M\"] chosen [0] at 178,144 32x40", "    list #12 multiple [\"a\", \"b\"] chosen [] at 12,190 160x96", "    combo #13 [\"x\"] chosen [0] at 178,190 80x24", "  progress #14 value 4 in 0..10 at 12,292 160x16", "  slider #15 value 1 in -5..5 at 12,314 160x24", "  picture #16 at 12,344 3x2", "  button #17 \"Go\" disabled at 12,352 40x24"]
    close(@app).must()

test "wait: every event kind a person gives, in the order injected"
    app @= fixture.test_app()
    w = fixture.window_of(app)
    b = button(@app, into: w, text: "OK").must()
    f = text_field(@app, into: w, text: "").must()
    a = text_area(@app, into: w, text: "").must()
    c = checkbox(@app, into: w, text: "On", checked: true).must()
    g = radio_group(@app, into: w, items: ["a", "b"]).must()
    l = list(@app, into: w, items: ["a", "b", "c"], multiple: false).must()
    k = combo(@app, into: w, items: ["x", "y"]).must()
    s = slider(@app, into: w, least: 0, most: 10, value: 0).must()
    m = menu(@app, into: w, title: "Edit").must()
    item = menu_item(@app, into: m, text: "Copy").must()
    inputs: [Input] = [
        .click(widget: b)
        .type_text(widget: f, text: "x")
        .type_text(widget: a, text: "a\nb")
        .toggle(widget: c)
        .choose(widget: g, indices: [1])
        .choose(widget: l, indices: [2])
        .choose(widget: k, indices: [1])
        .slide(widget: s, value: 7)
        .choose_menu(item: item)
        .ask_close(window: w)
        .resize(window: w, width: 900, height: 900)
    ]

    for input in inputs
        inject(@app, input: input).must()

    expected: [Event] = [
        .clicked(widget: b)
        .text_changed(widget: f, text: "x")
        .text_changed(widget: a, text: "a\nb")
        .toggled(widget: c, checked: false)
        .selection_changed(widget: g, indices: [1])
        .selection_changed(widget: l, indices: [2])
        .selection_changed(widget: k, indices: [1])
        .value_changed(widget: s, value: 7)
        .menu_chosen(item: item)
        .close_asked(window: w)
        .resized(window: w, width: 900, height: 900)
    ]

    for e in expected
        assert wait(@app, within: time.nanoseconds(0)).must() == e

    assert fixture.code_of(wait(@app, within: time.nanoseconds(0))) == CLOSED
    close(@app).must()

test "inject: what a person could not give makes no event: a hidden box's button, a choice that changes nothing, a slide to where it stands"
    app @= fixture.test_app()
    w = fixture.window_of(app)
    r = row(@app, into: w).must()
    b = button(@app, into: r, text: "In").must()
    set_visible(@app, widget: r, on: false).must()
    g = radio_group(@app, into: w, items: ["a", "b"]).must()
    s = slider(@app, into: w, least: 0, most: 10, value: 3).must()
    inject(@app, input: .click(widget: b)).must()
    inject(@app, input: .choose(widget: g, indices: [0])).must()
    inject(@app, input: .slide(widget: s, value: 3)).must()
    assert fixture.code_of(wait(@app, within: time.nanoseconds(0))) == CLOSED
    close(@app).must()

test "inject: an input on a widget destroyed before it is given makes no event, and an event of a widget destroyed is dropped"
    app @= fixture.test_app()
    w = fixture.window_of(app)
    gone = button(@app, into: w, text: "Gone").must()
    kept = button(@app, into: w, text: "Kept").must()
    inject(@app, input: .click(widget: gone)).must()
    inject(@app, input: .click(widget: kept)).must()
    destroy(@app, widget: gone).must()
    assert wait(@app, within: time.nanoseconds(0)).must() == .clicked(widget: kept)
    assert fixture.code_of(wait(@app, within: time.nanoseconds(0))) == CLOSED
    close(@app).must()

test "inject: refused as a value where the input does not fit its widget"
    app @= fixture.test_app()
    w = fixture.window_of(app)
    b = button(@app, into: w, text: "OK").must()
    f = text_field(@app, into: w, text: "").must()
    g = radio_group(@app, into: w, items: ["a", "b"]).must()
    l = list(@app, into: w, items: ["a", "b"], multiple: false).must()
    s = slider(@app, into: w, least: 0, most: 10, value: 0).must()
    assert fixture.code_of(inject(@app, input: .toggle(widget: b))) == INVALID
    assert fixture.code_of(inject(@app, input: .type_text(widget: f, text: "two\nlines"))) == INVALID
    assert fixture.code_of(inject(@app, input: .choose(widget: g, indices: []))) == INVALID
    assert fixture.code_of(inject(@app, input: .choose(widget: l, indices: [0, 1]))) == INVALID
    assert fixture.code_of(inject(@app, input: .choose(widget: l, indices: [2]))) == INVALID
    assert fixture.code_of(inject(@app, input: .slide(widget: s, value: 11))) == INVALID
    assert fixture.code_of(inject(@app, input: .resize(window: w, width: -1, height: 10))) == INVALID
    assert fixture.code_of(inject(@app, input: .pick_files(paths: []))) == INVALID
    assert fixture.code_of(inject(@app, input: .ask_close(window: b))) == INVALID
    close(@app).must()

test "destroy: every call on a widget of a destroyed window is closed, never a fault"
    app @= fixture.test_app()
    w = fixture.window_of(app)
    b = button(@app, into: w, text: "OK").must()
    l = list(@app, into: w, items: ["a"], multiple: false).must()
    destroy(@app, widget: w).must()
    assert fixture.code_of(set_text(@app, widget: b, text: "x")) == CLOSED
    assert fixture.code_of(text_of(app, widget: b)) == CLOSED
    assert fixture.code_of(selection_of(app, widget: l)) == CLOSED
    assert fixture.code_of(rect_of(@app, widget: b)) == CLOSED
    assert fixture.code_of(inject(@app, input: .click(widget: b))) == CLOSED
    assert fixture.code_of(destroy(@app, widget: w)) == CLOSED
    assert fixture.code_of(label(@app, into: w, text: "late")) == CLOSED
    assert render(@app).must().len() == 0
    close(@app).must()

test "close: every call on a closed app, and on a widget it made, is closed"
    app @= fixture.test_app()
    w = fixture.window_of(app)
    b = button(@app, into: w, text: "OK").must()
    close(@app).must()
    assert fixture.code_of(set_text(@app, widget: b, text: "x")) == CLOSED
    assert fixture.code_of(window(@app, title: "again", width: 10, height: 10)) == CLOSED
    assert fixture.code_of(wait(@app, within: time.nanoseconds(0))) == CLOSED
    assert fixture.code_of(render(@app)) == CLOSED
    assert fixture.code_of(close(@app)) == CLOSED

test "kind_of: a widget of another app is invalid, never another app's widget"
    first @= fixture.test_app()
    second @= fixture.test_app()
    b = button(@first, into: fixture.window_of(first), text: "OK").must()
    assert fixture.code_of(kind_of(second, widget: b)) == INVALID
    assert fixture.code_of(set_text(@second, widget: b, text: "x")) == INVALID
    close(@first).must()
    close(@second).must()

test "window: a title of two lines, a zero byte, sizes below zero and past the most, refused"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    assert fixture.code_of(window(@app, title: "a\nb", width: 10, height: 10)) == INVALID
    assert fixture.code_of(window(@app, title: bytes.to_text([97, 0]).must(), width: 10, height: 10)) == INVALID
    assert fixture.code_of(window(@app, title: "x", width: -1, height: 10)) == INVALID
    assert fixture.code_of(window(@app, title: "x", width: 10, height: MOST_SIZE + 1)) == INVALID
    assert !window(@app, title: "x", width: 0, height: MOST_SIZE).is_err()
    close(@app).must()

test "makers: a widget into a button, a menu into a box, an item into a window, a range upside down, a grid of no column, refused"
    app @= fixture.test_app()
    w = fixture.window_of(app)
    b = button(@app, into: w, text: "OK").must()
    r = row(@app, into: w).must()
    assert fixture.code_of(label(@app, into: b, text: "x")) == INVALID
    assert fixture.code_of(menu(@app, into: r, title: "File")) == INVALID
    assert fixture.code_of(menu_item(@app, into: w, text: "Open")) == INVALID
    assert fixture.code_of(slider(@app, into: w, least: 5, most: 5, value: 5)) == INVALID
    assert fixture.code_of(slider(@app, into: w, least: 0, most: 5, value: 6)) == INVALID
    assert fixture.code_of(progress(@app, into: w, most: 0)) == INVALID
    assert fixture.code_of(grid(@app, into: w, columns: 0)) == INVALID
    assert fixture.code_of(button(@app, into: w, text: "two\nlines")) == INVALID
    assert fixture.code_of(list(@app, into: w, items: ["a", "b\nc"], multiple: false)) == INVALID
    close(@app).must()

test "setters: a kind the setter does not set, and values out of range, refused and nothing changed"
    app @= fixture.test_app()
    w = fixture.window_of(app)
    b = button(@app, into: w, text: "OK").must()
    s = slider(@app, into: w, least: 0, most: 10, value: 5).must()
    l = list(@app, into: w, items: ["a", "b"], multiple: false).must()
    m = menu(@app, into: w, title: "File").must()
    assert fixture.code_of(set_checked(@app, widget: b, checked: true)) == INVALID
    assert fixture.code_of(set_value(@app, widget: s, value: 11)) == INVALID
    assert value_of(app, widget: s).must() == 5
    assert fixture.code_of(set_selection(@app, widget: l, indices: [1, 0])) == INVALID
    assert fixture.code_of(set_text(@app, widget: s, text: "x")) == INVALID
    assert fixture.code_of(set_fit(@app, widget: m, fit: default_fit())) == INVALID
    assert fixture.code_of(set_visible(@app, widget: m, on: false)) == INVALID
    assert fixture.code_of(set_enabled(@app, widget: w, on: false)) == INVALID
    assert fixture.code_of(set_range(@app, widget: s, least: 3, most: 2)) == INVALID
    bad @= default_fit()
    bad.stretch @ MOST_STRETCH + 1
    assert fixture.code_of(set_fit(@app, widget: b, fit: bad)) == INVALID
    assert fixture.code_of(set_shortcut(@app, item: m, shortcut: Shortcut(key: "o", primary: true, shift: false, alt: false))) == INVALID
    close(@app).must()

test "wait: a time below zero is invalid, and a headless app with nothing to give and no timer is closed at once"
    app @= fixture.test_app()
    assert fixture.code_of(wait(@app, within: time.nanoseconds(-1))) == INVALID
    assert fixture.code_of(wait(@app, within: time.max_duration())) == CLOSED
    close(@app).must()

test "timer: two timers fire in order of their times on the headless clock, a destroyed one never again"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    fast = timer(@app, every: time.milliseconds(300).must()).must()
    slow = timer(@app, every: time.milliseconds(500).must()).must()
    second = time.seconds(1).must()
    fired: [Widget] @= []

    for _ in range(from: 0, to: 4)
        match wait(@app, within: second).must()
            .timer_fired t => fired @ fired.push(t.timer)
            .clicked _ | .text_changed _ | .selection_changed _ | .toggled _ | .value_changed _ | .menu_chosen _ | .close_asked _ | .resized _ | .message _ | .moved _ | .state_changed _ | .screen_changed _ | .activated _ | .sort_asked _ | .unfolded _ | .submitted _ =>
                assert false

    assert fired == [fast, slow, fast, fast]
    destroy(@app, widget: fast).must()
    assert wait(@app, within: second).must() == .timer_fired(timer: slow)
    destroy(@app, widget: slow).must()
    assert fixture.code_of(wait(@app, within: second)) == CLOSED
    assert fixture.code_of(timer(@app, every: time.nanoseconds(10))) == INVALID
    close(@app).must()

test "wait: a timer due after the time given is a timeout, and fires on a later wait"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    t = timer(@app, every: time.seconds(5).must()).must()
    assert fixture.code_of(wait(@app, within: time.seconds(2).must())) == TIMEOUT
    assert fixture.code_of(wait(@app, within: time.seconds(2).must())) == TIMEOUT
    assert wait(@app, within: time.seconds(2).must()).must() == .timer_fired(timer: t)
    close(@app).must()

test "resize: a window asked smaller than its content needs keeps its need, and its widgets are laid out again"
    app @= fixture.test_app()
    w = fixture.window_of(app)
    f = text_field(@app, into: w, text: "").must()
    fit @= default_fit()
    fit.fill @ true
    set_fit(@app, widget: f, fit: fit).must()
    inject(@app, input: .resize(window: w, width: 10, height: 10)).must()
    assert wait(@app, within: time.nanoseconds(0)).must() == .resized(window: w, width: 184, height: 48)
    assert rect_of(@app, widget: f).must() == Rect(x: 12, y: 12, width: 160, height: 24)
    inject(@app, input: .resize(window: w, width: 400, height: 100)).must()
    _ = wait(@app, within: time.nanoseconds(0)).must()
    assert rect_of(@app, widget: f).must() == Rect(x: 12, y: 12, width: 376, height: 24)
    close(@app).must()

test "message: no answer injected on a headless app is closed, an answer that is no button of the box invalid"
    app @= fixture.test_app()
    w = fixture.window_of(app)
    assert fixture.code_of(message(@app, over: w, title: "Hi", text: "Hello", tone: .info, buttons: .ok)) == CLOSED
    inject(@app, input: .answer(answer: .yes)).must()
    assert fixture.code_of(message(@app, over: w, title: "Hi", text: "Hello", tone: .info, buttons: .ok_cancel)) == INVALID
    inject(@app, input: .answer(answer: .cancel)).must()
    assert message(@app, over: w, title: "Hi", text: "Hello", tone: .error, buttons: .ok_cancel).must() == .cancel
    close(@app).must()

test "open_files: several paths for a dialog of one, and none injected on a headless app, refused"
    app @= fixture.test_app()
    w = fixture.window_of(app)
    inject(@app, input: .pick_files(paths: ["/a", "/b"])).must()
    assert fixture.code_of(open_files(@app, over: w, title: "Open", filters: [], multiple: false)) == INVALID
    inject(@app, input: .pick_files(paths: ["/a", "/b"])).must()
    assert open_files(@app, over: w, title: "Open", filters: [], multiple: true).must() == .chosen(paths: ["/a", "/b"])
    assert fixture.code_of(save_file(@app, over: w, title: "Save", name: "x", filters: [Filter(name: "Text", extensions: ["txt"])])) == CLOSED
    assert fixture.code_of(open_files(@app, over: w, title: "Open", filters: [Filter(name: "Bad", extensions: [".txt"])], multiple: false)) == INVALID
    close(@app).must()

test "set_items: a combo box and a radio group refilled choose their first, emptied choose none"
    app @= fixture.test_app()
    w = fixture.window_of(app)
    c = combo(@app, into: w, items: ["a"]).must()
    set_items(@app, widget: c, items: ["x", "y"]).must()
    assert selection_of(app, widget: c).must() == [0]
    set_items(@app, widget: c, items: []).must()
    assert selection_of(app, widget: c).must().len() == 0
    close(@app).must()

test "set_text: a longer label widens its window's layout, and a text area keeps its lines"
    app @= fixture.test_app()
    w = fixture.window_of(app)
    l = label(@app, into: w, text: "a").must()
    assert rect_of(@app, widget: l).must().width == 8
    set_text(@app, widget: l, text: "a longer text").must()
    assert rect_of(@app, widget: l).must().width == 104
    set_text(@app, widget: w, text: "Renamed").must()
    assert text_of(app, widget: w).must() == "Renamed"
    close(@app).must()

## Examples

# A headless app opened, a window made in it, and the app closed.
test "open: a headless app with a window"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    assert !is_native(app)
    _ = window(@app, title: "Hello", width: 200, height: 80).must()
    close(@app).must()

# Closing an app twice: the second is told.
test "close: once, then again"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    close(@app).must()
    assert close(@app).is_err()

# The side an app stands on.
test "is_native: a headless app is not"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    assert !is_native(app)
    close(@app).must()

# What a widget is, and that a destroyed one is told.
test "kind_of: a button, then destroyed"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Kinds", width: 100, height: 50).must()
    b = button(@app, into: w, text: "OK").must()
    assert kind_of(app, widget: b).must() == .button
    destroy(@app, widget: b).must()

    match kind_of(app, widget: b)
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == CLOSED

    close(@app).must()

# A window destroyed with what it holds.
test "destroy: a window and its button"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Gone", width: 100, height: 50).must()
    b = button(@app, into: w, text: "OK").must()
    destroy(@app, widget: w).must()
    assert set_text(@app, widget: b, text: "Still?").is_err()
    close(@app).must()

# Where a button stands once laid out: inside the window's padding.
test "rect_of: a button in a window"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Where", width: 200, height: 100).must()
    b = button(@app, into: w, text: "OK").must()
    assert rect_of(@app, widget: b).must() == Rect(x: 12, y: 12, width: 40, height: 24)
    assert rect_of(@app, widget: w).must() == Rect(x: 0, y: 0, width: 200, height: 100)
    close(@app).must()

# A window and its title as text.
test "window: titled, and its size"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    _ = window(@app, title: "Notes", width: 320, height: 200).must()
    assert render(@app).must() == ["window #1 \"Notes\" 320x200"]
    close(@app).must()

# A window made bigger by the program.
test "set_size: a window grown"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Grow", width: 100, height: 50).must()
    set_size(@app, widget: w, width: 300, height: 150).must()
    assert rect_of(@app, widget: w).must() == Rect(x: 0, y: 0, width: 300, height: 150)
    close(@app).must()

# A window the person may not resize: an injected resize gives no event.
test "set_resizable: a fixed window"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Fixed", width: 100, height: 50).must()
    set_resizable(@app, widget: w, on: false).must()
    inject(@app, input: .resize(window: w, width: 300, height: 300)).must()
    assert wait(@app, within: time.nanoseconds(0)).is_err()
    assert render(@app).must() == ["window #1 \"Fixed\" 100x50 fixed"]
    close(@app).must()

# Two buttons side by side.
test "row: two buttons"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Row", width: 200, height: 60).must()
    r = row(@app, into: w).must()
    _ = button(@app, into: r, text: "Yes").must()
    _ = button(@app, into: r, text: "No").must()
    assert render(@app).must()[3] == "    button #4 \"No\" at 66,12 40x24"
    close(@app).must()

# A label over a text field.
test "column: a label over a field"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Column", width: 200, height: 100).must()
    c = column(@app, into: w).must()
    _ = label(@app, into: c, text: "Name").must()
    f = text_field(@app, into: c, text: "").must()
    assert rect_of(@app, widget: f).must() == Rect(x: 12, y: 34, width: 160, height: 24)
    close(@app).must()

# Labels and fields in a grid of two columns.
test "grid: a form of two rows"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Form", width: 300, height: 100).must()
    g = grid(@app, into: w, columns: 2).must()
    _ = label(@app, into: g, text: "Name").must()
    _ = text_field(@app, into: g, text: "").must()
    _ = label(@app, into: g, text: "Mail").must()
    mail = text_field(@app, into: g, text: "").must()
    assert rect_of(@app, widget: mail).must() == Rect(x: 50, y: 42, width: 160, height: 24)
    close(@app).must()

# The fit a widget starts with.
test "default_fit: no stretch, no fill"
    f = default_fit()
    assert f.stretch == 0 && !f.fill && f.align == .start

# A text field stretched to the width the row leaves.
test "set_fit: a field that takes the room left"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Fit", width: 400, height: 60).must()
    r = row(@app, into: w).must()
    f = text_field(@app, into: r, text: "").must()
    _ = button(@app, into: r, text: "Go").must()
    fit @= default_fit()
    fit.stretch @ 1
    set_fit(@app, widget: f, fit: fit).must()
    fill @= default_fit()
    fill.fill @ true
    set_fit(@app, widget: r, fit: fill).must()
    assert rect_of(@app, widget: f).must() == Rect(x: 12, y: 12, width: 330, height: 24)
    close(@app).must()

# A window with no padding and no spacing.
test "set_spacing: a window packed tight"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Tight", width: 100, height: 100).must()
    set_spacing(@app, box: w, spacing: 0, padding: 0).must()
    b = button(@app, into: w, text: "A").must()
    assert rect_of(@app, widget: b).must() == Rect(x: 0, y: 0, width: 32, height: 24)
    close(@app).must()

# A grid whose second column takes the room left.
test "set_grid_stretch: the fields column grows"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Grow", width: 400, height: 60).must()
    g = grid(@app, into: w, columns: 2).must()
    fit @= default_fit()
    fit.fill @ true
    set_fit(@app, widget: g, fit: fit).must()
    set_grid_stretch(@app, widget: g, columns: [0, 1], rows: []).must()
    _ = label(@app, into: g, text: "Name").must()
    f = text_field(@app, into: g, text: "").must()
    set_fit(@app, widget: f, fit: fit).must()
    assert rect_of(@app, widget: f).must() == Rect(x: 50, y: 12, width: 338, height: 24)
    close(@app).must()

# A label of two lines.
test "label: two lines"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Label", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    l = label(@app, into: w, text: "one\ntwo").must()
    assert rect_of(@app, widget: l).must() == Rect(x: 12, y: 12, width: 24, height: 32)
    close(@app).must()

# A button clicked by an injected input.
test "button: clicked"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Click", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    b = button(@app, into: w, text: "OK").must()
    inject(@app, input: .click(widget: b)).must()
    assert wait(@app, within: time.nanoseconds(0)).must() == .clicked(widget: b)
    close(@app).must()

# A field the person types into.
test "text_field: typed into"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Type", width: 200, height: 60).must()
    f = text_field(@app, into: w, text: "").must()
    inject(@app, input: .type_text(widget: f, text: "hello")).must()
    assert wait(@app, within: time.nanoseconds(0)).must() == .text_changed(widget: f, text: "hello")
    assert text_of(app, widget: f).must() == "hello"
    close(@app).must()

# An area holding lines.
test "text_area: two lines"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Area", width: 300, height: 200).must()
    a = text_area(@app, into: w, text: "first\nsecond").must()
    assert text_of(app, widget: a).must() == "first\nsecond"
    close(@app).must()

# A checkbox toggled by the person.
test "checkbox: toggled"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Check", width: 200, height: 60).must()
    c = checkbox(@app, into: w, text: "Wrap", checked: false).must()
    inject(@app, input: .toggle(widget: c)).must()
    assert wait(@app, within: time.nanoseconds(0)).must() == .toggled(widget: c, checked: true)
    assert is_checked(app, widget: c).must()
    close(@app).must()

# A radio group, its first item chosen, the person choosing the third.
test "radio_group: a size chosen"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Size", width: 200, height: 100).must()
    g = radio_group(@app, into: w, items: ["Small", "Medium", "Large"]).must()
    assert selection_of(app, widget: g).must() == [0]
    inject(@app, input: .choose(widget: g, indices: [2])).must()
    assert wait(@app, within: time.nanoseconds(0)).must() == .selection_changed(widget: g, indices: [2])
    close(@app).must()

# A list of several choices.
test "list: two chosen"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "List", width: 200, height: 150).must()
    l = list(@app, into: w, items: ["a", "b", "c"], multiple: true).must()
    assert selection_of(app, widget: l).must().len() == 0
    inject(@app, input: .choose(widget: l, indices: [0, 2])).must()
    assert wait(@app, within: time.nanoseconds(0)).must() == .selection_changed(widget: l, indices: [0, 2])
    close(@app).must()

# A combo box, its first item chosen.
test "combo: a day"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Day", width: 200, height: 60).must()
    c = combo(@app, into: w, items: ["Monday", "Tuesday"]).must()
    assert selection_of(app, widget: c).must() == [0]
    close(@app).must()

# A progress bar filled half way.
test "progress: half way"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Work", width: 200, height: 60).must()
    p = progress(@app, into: w, most: 10).must()
    set_value(@app, widget: p, value: 5).must()
    assert value_of(app, widget: p).must() == 5
    close(@app).must()

# A slider moved by the person.
test "slider: dragged"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Volume", width: 200, height: 60).must()
    s = slider(@app, into: w, least: 0, most: 100, value: 50).must()
    inject(@app, input: .slide(widget: s, value: 80)).must()
    assert wait(@app, within: time.nanoseconds(0)).must() == .value_changed(widget: s, value: 80)
    close(@app).must()

# A picture of a decoded image, which takes its size.
test "picture: two by one"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Picture", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    img = image.blank(width: 2, height: 1, format: .rgb).must()
    p = picture(@app, into: w, img: img).must()
    assert rect_of(@app, widget: p).must() == Rect(x: 12, y: 12, width: 2, height: 1)
    close(@app).must()

# A label's text changed by the program.
test "set_text: a label"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Text", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    l = label(@app, into: w, text: "Ready").must()
    set_text(@app, widget: l, text: "Done").must()
    assert text_of(app, widget: l).must() == "Done"
    close(@app).must()

# The text a field holds.
test "text_of: a field's first text"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Text", width: 200, height: 60).must()
    f = text_field(@app, into: w, text: "abc").must()
    assert text_of(app, widget: f).must() == "abc"
    close(@app).must()

# A button greyed out: a click on it gives nothing.
test "set_enabled: a button greyed out"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Off", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    b = button(@app, into: w, text: "OK").must()
    set_enabled(@app, widget: b, on: false).must()
    inject(@app, input: .click(widget: b)).must()

    match wait(@app, within: time.nanoseconds(0))
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == CLOSED

    close(@app).must()

# A hidden label takes no room.
test "set_visible: a label hidden"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Hide", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    l = label(@app, into: w, text: "Gone").must()
    b = button(@app, into: w, text: "OK").must()
    set_visible(@app, widget: l, on: false).must()
    assert rect_of(@app, widget: b).must() == Rect(x: 12, y: 12, width: 40, height: 24)
    close(@app).must()

# A checkbox checked by the program.
test "set_checked: checked"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Check", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    c = checkbox(@app, into: w, text: "On", checked: false).must()
    set_checked(@app, widget: c, checked: true).must()
    assert is_checked(app, widget: c).must()
    close(@app).must()

# Whether a checkbox is checked.
test "is_checked: as made"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Check", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    c = checkbox(@app, into: w, text: "On", checked: true).must()
    assert is_checked(app, widget: c).must()
    close(@app).must()

# A slider moved by the program.
test "set_value: a slider"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Value", width: 200, height: 60).must()
    s = slider(@app, into: w, least: -10, most: 10, value: 0).must()
    set_value(@app, widget: s, value: -10).must()
    assert value_of(app, widget: s).must() == -10
    close(@app).must()

# Where a slider stands.
test "value_of: a slider as made"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Value", width: 200, height: 60).must()
    s = slider(@app, into: w, least: 0, most: 10, value: 3).must()
    assert value_of(app, widget: s).must() == 3
    close(@app).must()

# A slider's range narrowed, its value moved inside it.
test "set_range: narrowed"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Range", width: 200, height: 60).must()
    s = slider(@app, into: w, least: 0, most: 100, value: 90).must()
    set_range(@app, widget: s, least: 0, most: 50).must()
    assert value_of(app, widget: s).must() == 50
    close(@app).must()

# A list given new items, nothing chosen.
test "set_items: a list refilled"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Items", width: 200, height: 150).must()
    l = list(@app, into: w, items: ["a"], multiple: false).must()
    set_items(@app, widget: l, items: ["x", "y"]).must()
    assert selection_of(app, widget: l).must().len() == 0
    close(@app).must()

# An item chosen by the program.
test "set_selection: the second"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Choose", width: 200, height: 150).must()
    l = list(@app, into: w, items: ["a", "b"], multiple: false).must()
    set_selection(@app, widget: l, indices: [1]).must()
    assert selection_of(app, widget: l).must() == [1]
    close(@app).must()

# What a radio group has chosen.
test "selection_of: a radio group as made"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Choose", width: 200, height: 100).must()
    g = radio_group(@app, into: w, items: ["a", "b"]).must()
    assert selection_of(app, widget: g).must() == [0]
    close(@app).must()

# A picture given a bigger image.
test "set_picture: a bigger image"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Picture", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    p = picture(@app, into: w, img: image.blank(width: 1, height: 1, format: .gray).must()).must()
    set_picture(@app, widget: p, img: image.blank(width: 4, height: 3, format: .rgba).must()).must()
    assert rect_of(@app, widget: p).must().width == 4
    close(@app).must()

# A menu bar with a File menu.
test "menu: File"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Menus", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    _ = menu(@app, into: w, title: "File").must()
    assert render(@app).must()[1] == "  menu #2 \"File\""
    close(@app).must()

# An item chosen from a menu.
test "menu_item: chosen"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Menus", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    m = menu(@app, into: w, title: "File").must()
    item = menu_item(@app, into: m, text: "Open").must()
    inject(@app, input: .choose_menu(item: item)).must()
    assert wait(@app, within: time.nanoseconds(0)).must() == .menu_chosen(item: item)
    close(@app).must()

# A line between two items.
test "menu_separator: between two items"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Menus", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    m = menu(@app, into: w, title: "File").must()
    _ = menu_item(@app, into: m, text: "Open").must()
    _ = menu_separator(@app, into: m).must()
    assert render(@app).must()[3] == "    menu_separator #4"
    close(@app).must()

# Command-O on macOS, Control-O elsewhere.
test "set_shortcut: the primary key and o"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Menus", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    m = menu(@app, into: w, title: "File").must()
    item = menu_item(@app, into: m, text: "Open").must()
    set_shortcut(@app, item: item, shortcut: Shortcut(key: "o", primary: true, shift: false, alt: false)).must()
    assert render(@app).must()[2] == "    menu_item #3 \"Open\" primary+o"
    close(@app).must()

# The About item of a Help menu, which macOS shows in the application's menu.
test "set_role: About in the Help menu"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Menus", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    help = menu(@app, into: w, title: "Help").must()
    about = menu_item(@app, into: help, text: "About Notes").must()
    set_role(@app, item: about, role: .about).must()
    assert render(@app).must()[2] == "    menu_item #3 \"About Notes\" role about"
    inject(@app, input: .choose_menu(item: about)).must()
    assert wait(@app, within: time.nanoseconds(0)).must() == .menu_chosen(item: about)
    close(@app).must()

# A menu inside the File menu.
test "menu: Open Recent inside File"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Menus", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    file = menu(@app, into: w, title: "File").must()
    recent = menu(@app, into: file, title: "Open Recent").must()
    item = menu_item(@app, into: recent, text: "notes.txt").must()
    inject(@app, input: .choose_menu(item: item)).must()
    assert wait(@app, within: time.nanoseconds(0)).must() == .menu_chosen(item: item)
    close(@app).must()

# View > Show Sidebar, turned off by the person.
test "check_item: Show Sidebar"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Menus", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    view = menu(@app, into: w, title: "View").must()
    sidebar = check_item(@app, into: view, text: "Show Sidebar", checked: true).must()
    inject(@app, input: .choose_menu(item: sidebar)).must()
    _ = wait(@app, within: time.nanoseconds(0)).must()
    assert !is_checked(app, widget: sidebar).must()
    close(@app).must()

# View > as Icons or as List, the second chosen.
test "radio_item: as Icons, as List"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Menus", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    view = menu(@app, into: w, title: "View").must()
    icons = radio_item(@app, into: view, text: "as Icons", checked: true).must()
    as_list = radio_item(@app, into: view, text: "as List", checked: false).must()
    inject(@app, input: .choose_menu(item: as_list)).must()
    _ = wait(@app, within: time.nanoseconds(0)).must()
    assert is_checked(app, widget: as_list).must() && !is_checked(app, widget: icons).must()
    close(@app).must()

# A password typed into a field that masks it.
test "password_field: typed into"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Sign in", width: 200, height: 60).must()
    secret = password_field(@app, into: w, text: "").must()
    inject(@app, input: .type_text(widget: secret, text: "hunter2")).must()
    _ = wait(@app, within: time.nanoseconds(0)).must()
    assert text_of(app, widget: secret).must() == "hunter2"
    close(@app).must()

# A copy whose end is unknown.
test "set_moving: a copy of unknown length"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Copy", width: 200, height: 60).must()
    bar = progress(@app, into: w, most: 100).must()
    set_moving(@app, widget: bar, on: true).must()
    assert render(@app).must()[1] == "  progress #2 value 0 in 0..100 moving at 12,12 160x16"
    close(@app).must()

# A timer firing on a headless app's clock.
test "timer: every second"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    t = timer(@app, every: time.seconds(1).must()).must()
    assert wait(@app, within: time.seconds(2).must()).must() == .timer_fired(timer: t)
    close(@app).must()

# The loop of a program: wait, then match what the person did.
test "wait: one click, then the inputs are spent"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Loop", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    b = button(@app, into: w, text: "OK").must()
    inject(@app, input: .click(widget: b)).must()
    clicks @= 0

    while true
        match wait(@app, within: time.milliseconds(100).must())
            .ok event =>
                if event == .clicked(widget: b)
                    clicks @ clicks + 1
            .err e =>
                assert e.code == CLOSED
                break

    assert clicks == 1
    close(@app).must()

# The close button pressed, which the program answers by destroying the
# window.
test "inject: the close button"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Bye", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    inject(@app, input: .ask_close(window: w)).must()
    assert wait(@app, within: time.nanoseconds(0)).must() == .close_asked(window: w)
    destroy(@app, widget: w).must()
    close(@app).must()

# A menu item chosen by its shortcut, the program pressing its keys.
test "press_key: the primary key and o"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Keys", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    file = menu(@app, into: w, title: "File").must()
    item = menu_item(@app, into: file, text: "Open").must()
    o = Shortcut(key: "o", primary: true, shift: false, alt: false)
    set_shortcut(@app, item: item, shortcut: o).must()
    press_key(@app, widget: w, shortcut: o).must()
    assert wait(@app, within: time.nanoseconds(0)).must() == .menu_chosen(item: item)
    close(@app).must()

# The screens a headless app reports: its one, the same on every machine.
test "screens: the headless side's one"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    assert screens(app).must().len() == 1
    assert screens(app).must()[0].scale == 100
    close(@app).must()

# Where a new window stands on the headless side: the middle of the work
# area, its title bar above its content.
test "frame_of: a new window"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Here", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    assert frame_of(@app, widget: w).must() == Frame(screen: 0, x: 910, y: 476, width: 100, height: 88)
    close(@app).must()

# A window put at the top-left corner of the main screen.
test "set_position: the corner of the screen"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Here", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    set_position(@app, widget: w, screen: 0, x: 0, y: 0).must()
    assert frame_of(@app, widget: w).must().x == 0
    close(@app).must()

# A window moved away, then centred again.
test "center: back in the middle"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Here", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    set_position(@app, widget: w, screen: 0, x: 0, y: 0).must()
    center(@app, widget: w).must()
    assert frame_of(@app, widget: w).must().x == 910
    close(@app).must()

# A dialog centred over its document.
test "center_over: a dialog over its document"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    document = window(@app, title: "Notes", width: 400, height: 300).must()
    set_position(@app, widget: document, screen: 0, x: 100, y: 100).must()
    dialog = window(@app, title: "Find", width: 200, height: 100).must()
    center_over(@app, widget: dialog, over: document).must()
    assert frame_of(@app, widget: dialog).must().x == 200
    close(@app).must()

# A window maximized over the work area.
test "set_state: maximized"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Big", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    set_state(@app, widget: w, to: .maximized).must()
    assert state_of(app, widget: w).must() == .maximized
    close(@app).must()

# A new window stands in the normal state.
test "state_of: as made"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "New", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    assert state_of(app, widget: w).must() == .normal
    close(@app).must()

# A timer's window kept above the others.
test "set_on_top: a timer that floats"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Timer", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    set_on_top(@app, widget: w, on: true).must()
    assert render(@app).must()[0] == "window #1 \"Timer\" 100x60 on_top"
    close(@app).must()

# A panel with no title bar.
test "set_framed: a panel with no frame"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Panel", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    set_framed(@app, widget: w, on: false).must()
    assert frame_of(@app, widget: w).must().height == 60
    close(@app).must()

# An overlay half see-through.
test "set_opacity: half see-through"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Overlay", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    set_opacity(@app, widget: w, opacity: 128).must()
    assert render(@app).must()[0] == "window #1 \"Overlay\" 100x60 opacity 128"
    close(@app).must()

# A sidebar on the system's translucent material.
test "set_backdrop: translucent"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Sidebar", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    set_backdrop(@app, widget: w, backdrop: .translucent).must()
    assert render(@app).must()[0] == "window #1 \"Sidebar\" 100x60 translucent"
    close(@app).must()

# A settings window of two pages, the second chosen by its tab.
test "tabs: two pages of settings"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Settings", width: 320, height: 200).must()
    pages = tabs(@app, into: w).must()
    _ = page(@app, into: pages, title: "General").must()
    _ = page(@app, into: pages, title: "Advanced").must()
    inject(@app, input: .choose(widget: pages, indices: [1])).must()
    assert wait(@app, within: time.nanoseconds(0)).must() == .selection_changed(widget: pages, indices: [1])
    close(@app).must()

# A page's own widgets, laid out from its top-left corner.
test "page: a field on a page"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Settings", width: 320, height: 200).must()
    general = page(@app, into: tabs(@app, into: w).must(), title: "General").must()
    name = text_field(@app, into: general, text: "Ada").must()
    assert rect_of(@app, widget: name).must().x == 12
    close(@app).must()

# Thirty lines in a box of a hundred points, which scrolls.
test "scrolled: thirty lines"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Log", width: 200, height: 100).must()
    log = scrolled(@app, into: w).must()

    for i in range(from: 0, to: 30)
        _ = label(@app, into: log, text: f"line {i}").must()

    assert rect_of(@app, widget: log).must().height == 100
    close(@app).must()

# A sidebar beside the files, its divider dragged by the person.
test "split: a sidebar"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Files", width: 400, height: 200).must()
    sides = split(@app, into: w, side_by_side: true).must()
    set_fit(
        @app,
        widget: sides,
        fit: Fit(stretch: 1, fill: true, align: .start, min_width: 0, min_height: 0, margin: 0)
    ).must()
    _ = pane(@app, into: sides).must()
    _ = pane(@app, into: sides).must()
    inject(@app, input: .slide(widget: sides, value: 120)).must()
    assert wait(@app, within: time.nanoseconds(0)).must() == .value_changed(widget: sides, value: 120)
    close(@app).must()

# A list in the left pane.
test "pane: a list on the left"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Files", width: 400, height: 200).must()
    sides = split(@app, into: w, side_by_side: true).must()
    places = list(@app, into: pane(@app, into: sides).must(), items: ["Home", "Desktop"], multiple: false).must()
    _ = pane(@app, into: sides).must()
    assert rect_of(@app, widget: places).must().x == 0
    close(@app).must()

# A file manager's details, sorted by size where the person asks.
test "table: name and size"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Files", width: 300, height: 200).must()
    files = table(@app, into: w, columns: ["Name", "Size"]).must()
    set_rows(@app, widget: files, rows: [["notes.txt", "12"], ["plan.md", "3"]]).must()
    inject(@app, input: .sort(widget: files, column: 1, ascending: true)).must()
    assert wait(@app, within: time.nanoseconds(0)).must() == .sort_asked(widget: files, column: 1, ascending: true)
    set_rows(@app, widget: files, rows: [["plan.md", "3"], ["notes.txt", "12"]]).must()
    close(@app).must()

# Rows given, then read back.
test "set_rows: two files"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Files", width: 300, height: 200).must()
    files = table(@app, into: w, columns: ["Name"]).must()
    set_rows(@app, widget: files, rows: [["a.txt"], ["b.txt"]]).must()
    assert rows_of(app, widget: files).must() == [["a.txt"], ["b.txt"]]
    close(@app).must()

# The rows of a table as the program holds them.
test "rows_of: none yet"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Files", width: 300, height: 200).must()
    files = table(@app, into: w, columns: ["Name"]).must()
    assert rows_of(app, widget: files).must() == []
    close(@app).must()

# A table shown sorted by its first column, descending.
test "set_sort: by name, descending"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Files", width: 300, height: 200).must()
    files = table(@app, into: w, columns: ["Name", "Size"]).must()
    set_sort(@app, widget: files, order: .by(column: 0, ascending: false)).must()
    assert sort_of(app, widget: files).must() == .by(column: 0, ascending: false)
    close(@app).must()

# A table no one sorted.
test "sort_of: unsorted"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Files", width: 300, height: 200).must()
    files = table(@app, into: w, columns: ["Name"]).must()
    assert sort_of(app, widget: files).must() == .unsorted
    close(@app).must()

# A file's Open and Delete, shown where the person right-clicks the list.
test "context_menu: a file's actions"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Files", width: 200, height: 120).must()
    files = list(@app, into: w, items: ["notes.txt"], multiple: false).must()
    actions = context_menu(@app, widget: files).must()
    open_file = menu_item(@app, into: actions, text: "Open").must()
    _ = menu_item(@app, into: actions, text: "Delete").must()
    inject(@app, input: .choose_menu(item: open_file)).must()
    assert wait(@app, within: time.nanoseconds(0)).must() == .menu_chosen(item: open_file)
    close(@app).must()

# A disk's folders, loaded as the person unfolds them.
test "tree: folders loaded when unfolded"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Folders", width: 200, height: 200).must()
    folders = tree(@app, into: w).must()
    home = add_item(@app, widget: folders, under: 0, text: "Home", foldable: true).must()
    inject(@app, input: .unfold(widget: folders, item: home, open: true)).must()
    assert wait(@app, within: time.nanoseconds(0)).must() == .unfolded(widget: folders, item: home, open: true)
    _ = add_item(@app, widget: folders, under: home, text: "Desktop", foldable: true).must()
    close(@app).must()

# Two folders under a third.
test "add_item: two under one"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Folders", width: 200, height: 200).must()
    folders = tree(@app, into: w).must()
    home = add_item(@app, widget: folders, under: 0, text: "Home", foldable: false).must()
    _ = add_item(@app, widget: folders, under: home, text: "Desktop", foldable: false).must()
    _ = add_item(@app, widget: folders, under: home, text: "Music", foldable: false).must()
    assert items_under(app, widget: folders, item: home).must().len() == 2
    close(@app).must()

# A folder deleted with what it held.
test "remove_item: a folder and its own"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Folders", width: 200, height: 200).must()
    folders = tree(@app, into: w).must()
    home = add_item(@app, widget: folders, under: 0, text: "Home", foldable: false).must()
    _ = add_item(@app, widget: folders, under: home, text: "Desktop", foldable: false).must()
    remove_item(@app, widget: folders, item: home).must()
    assert items_under(app, widget: folders, item: 0).must() == []
    close(@app).must()

# The top of a tree.
test "items_under: the top"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Folders", width: 200, height: 200).must()
    folders = tree(@app, into: w).must()
    home = add_item(@app, widget: folders, under: 0, text: "Home", foldable: false).must()
    assert items_under(app, widget: folders, item: 0).must() == [home]
    close(@app).must()

# An item's text read back.
test "item_text: Home"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Folders", width: 200, height: 200).must()
    folders = tree(@app, into: w).must()
    home = add_item(@app, widget: folders, under: 0, text: "Home", foldable: true).must()
    assert item_text(app, widget: folders, item: home).must() == "Home"
    close(@app).must()

# A folder unfolded by the program.
test "set_unfolded: Home opened"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Folders", width: 200, height: 200).must()
    folders = tree(@app, into: w).must()
    home = add_item(@app, widget: folders, under: 0, text: "Home", foldable: true).must()
    set_unfolded(@app, widget: folders, item: home, on: true).must()
    assert is_unfolded(app, widget: folders, item: home).must()
    close(@app).must()

# A folder folded as it was made.
test "is_unfolded: folded at first"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Folders", width: 200, height: 200).must()
    folders = tree(@app, into: w).must()
    home = add_item(@app, widget: folders, under: 0, text: "Home", foldable: true).must()
    assert !is_unfolded(app, widget: folders, item: home).must()
    close(@app).must()

# Paths copied as a file manager copies them, and read back by a Paste.
test "set_clipboard: two files copied"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    set_clipboard(@app, clip: .files(paths: ["/Users/ada/notes.txt", "/Users/ada/plan.md"])).must()
    assert clipboard(app, kind: .files).must() == .files(paths: ["/Users/ada/notes.txt", "/Users/ada/plan.md"])
    close(@app).must()

# A Paste of text where the clipboard holds an image finds nothing.
test "clipboard: text asked, an image held"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    set_clipboard(@app, clip: .picture(img: image.blank(width: 2, height: 2, format: .rgba).must())).must()
    assert clipboard(app, kind: .text).must() == .nothing
    close(@app).must()

# The app's icon, a blank square.
test "set_icon: a blank square"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    set_icon(@app, img: image.blank(width: 32, height: 32, format: .rgba).must()).must()
    close(@app).must()

# A dialog the person answers before any other window.
test "set_modal: a dialog"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    document = window(@app, title: "Notes", width: 200, height: 100).must()
    dialog = window(@app, title: "Save?", width: 120, height: 60).must()
    set_modal(@app, widget: dialog, on: true).must()
    inject(@app, input: .ask_close(window: document)).must()
    inject(@app, input: .ask_close(window: dialog)).must()
    assert wait(@app, within: time.nanoseconds(0)).must() == .close_asked(window: dialog)
    close(@app).must()

# The caret put in a search field.
test "set_focus: the search field"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Find", width: 200, height: 80).must()
    field = text_field(@app, into: w, text: "").must()
    set_focus(@app, widget: field).must()
    assert focus_of(app, widget: w).must() == field
    close(@app).must()

# A new window's widgets hold no keys until one is given them.
test "focus_of: a new window"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Find", width: 200, height: 80).must()
    _ = text_field(@app, into: w, text: "").must()
    assert focus_of(app, widget: w).must() == w
    close(@app).must()

# A dialog's OK pressed by Return wherever its window has the keys.
test "set_button_key: OK by Return"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Ask", width: 160, height: 80).must()
    ok_button = button(@app, into: w, text: "OK").must()
    set_button_key(@app, widget: ok_button, key: .enter).must()
    press_key(@app, widget: w, shortcut: Shortcut(key: "Enter", primary: false, shift: false, alt: false)).must()
    assert wait(@app, within: time.nanoseconds(0)).must() == .clicked(widget: ok_button)
    close(@app).must()

# A folder chosen for a project.
test "open_folders: one chosen"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Project", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    inject(@app, input: .pick_files(paths: ["/tmp/project"])).must()
    assert open_folders(@app, over: w, title: "Choose the project's folder", multiple: false).must() == .chosen(paths: ["/tmp/project"])
    close(@app).must()

# Save, Don't Save or Cancel before a document closes, the person choosing
# the second.
test "ask: Save, Don't Save, Cancel"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Notes", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    inject(@app, input: .answer(answer: .button(index: 1))).must()
    pressed = ask(
        @app,
        over: w,
        title: "Save the changes?",
        text: "They are lost otherwise.",
        tone: .warning,
        buttons: ["Save", "Don't Save", "Cancel"],
        cancel: 2
    ).must()
    assert pressed == 1
    close(@app).must()

# A question answered Yes by an injected answer.
test "message: answered yes"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Ask", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    inject(@app, input: .answer(answer: .yes)).must()
    assert message(@app, over: w, title: "Save?", text: "Save the changes?", tone: .warning, buttons: .yes_no).must() == .yes
    close(@app).must()

# A file chosen to open.
test "open_files: one chosen"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Open", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    inject(@app, input: .pick_files(paths: ["/tmp/a.txt"])).must()
    chosen = open_files(
        @app,
        over: w,
        title: "Open",
        filters: [Filter(name: "Text", extensions: ["txt"])],
        multiple: false
    ).must()
    assert chosen == .chosen(paths: ["/tmp/a.txt"])
    close(@app).must()

# A save dialog cancelled.
test "save_file: cancelled"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Save", width: 100, height: 60).must()
    inject(@app, input: .cancel_files).must()
    assert save_file(@app, over: w, title: "Save", name: "notes.txt", filters: []).must() == .cancelled
    close(@app).must()

# A small window drawn as text.
test "render: a window with a label and a button"
    app @= open(.headless).must()
    w = window(@app, title: "Hi", width: 200, height: 80).must()
    _ = label(@app, into: w, text: "Hello").must()
    _ = button(@app, into: w, text: "OK").must()
    lines = render(@app).must()
    assert lines == ["window #1 \"Hi\" 200x80", "  label #2 \"Hello\" at 12,12 40x16", "  button #3 \"OK\" at 12,34 40x24"]
    close(@app).must()
